"Inspect workspace and app layout"},"hookAdditionalContexts":[],"toolCallId":"call-9f141dca-5940-45f5-aad9-96f69f1e406f-1\nfc_7f8049a5-599a-97e0-a25d-7da3d4943cb1_1","startedAtMs":"1787424322843","completedAtMs":"1787424323188"},"model_call_id":"507115f0-fb9b-4c24-8b3e-645abf4e560c-0-1r9m","session_id":"15abacc6-9531-4e57-8c31-24c1bdbac2cf","timestamp_ms":1787424323208}
{"type":"tool_call","subtype":"completed","call_id":"call-9f141dca-5940-45f5-aad9-96f69f1e406f-0\nfc_7f8049a5-599a-97e0-a25d-7da3d4943cb1_0","tool_call":{"shellToolCall":{"args":{"command":"git merge --no-edit main && loom data show MARATHON-2 --output json && loom data comments MARATHON-2 --output json 2>/dev/null || loom data show MARATHON-2 --output json | head -c 50000","workingDirectory":"","timeout":30000,"toolCallId":"call-9f141dca-5940-45f5-aad9-96f69f1e406f-0\nfc_7f8049a5-599a-97e0-a25d-7da3d4943cb1_0","simpleCommands":["git","loom","loom","loom","head"],"hasInputRedirect":false,"hasOutputRedirect":true,"parsingResult":{"parsingFailed":false,"executableCommands":[{"name":"git","args":[{"type":"word","value":"merge"},{"type":"word","value":"--no-edit"},{"type":"word","value":"main"}],"fullText":"git merge --no-edit main"},{"name":"loom","args":[{"type":"word","value":"data"},{"type":"word","value":"show"},{"type":"word","value":"MARATHON-2"},{"type":"word","value":"--output"},{"type":"word","value":"json"}],"fullText":"loom data show MARATHON-2 --output json"},{"name":"loom","args":[{"type":"word","value":"data"},{"type":"word","value":"comments"},{"type":"word","value":"MARATHON-2"},{"type":"word","value":"--output"},{"type":"word","value":"json"}],"fullText":"loom data comments MARATHON-2 --output json"},{"name":"loom","args":[{"type":"word","value":"data"},{"type":"word","value":"show"},{"type":"word","value":"MARATHON-2"},{"type":"word","value":"--output"},{"type":"word","value":"json"}],"fullText":"loom data show MARATHON-2 --output json"},{"name":"head","args":[{"type":"word","value":"-c"},{"type":"number","value":"50000"}],"fullText":"head -c 50000"}],"hasRedirects":true,"hasCommandSubstitution":false,"allRedirectsAreDevNull":true,"redirects":[{"operator":">","destinationFds":[2],"targetNodeType":"word","targetText":"/dev/null"}]},"fileOutputThresholdBytes":"40000","isBackground":false,"skipApproval":false,"timeoutBehavior":"TIMEOUT_BEHAVIOR_BACKGROUND","hardTimeout":86400000,"description":"Merge main and show task details","closeStdin":true,"conversationId":"15abacc6-9531-4e57-8c31-24c1bdbac2cf","adminCommandDenylist":[]},"result":{"success":{"command":"git merge --no-edit main && loom data show MARATHON-2 --output json && loom data comments MARATHON-2 --output json 2>/dev/null || loom data show MARATHON-2 --output json | head -c 50000","workingDirectory":"","exitCode":0,"signal":"","stdout":"Already up to date.\n{\n  \"id\": \"MARATHON-2\",\n  \"title\": \"Cluster scaffold: start.sh, 3 HTTP nodes, Redis, health\",\n  \"status\": \"in_progress\",\n  \"priority\": 0,\n  \"issue_type\": \"task\",\n  \"assignee\": \"frontend-dev-1\",\n  \"owner\": \"marathon-harness\",\n  \"labels\": [\n    \"backend\"\n  ],\n  \"source_repo\": \"app\",\n  \"parent\": \"MARATHON-1\",\n  \"design\": \"## Summary\\n\\nDeliver a bootable Huddle cluster skeleton at `/app`: a foreground `/app/start.sh` that launches local Redis on `127.0.0.1:6379` and three identical HTTP nodes on `127.0.0.1:8000/8001/8002`, plus `GET /api/health` returning `200 {\\\"status\\\":\\\"ok\\\",\\\"node_id\\\":\\u003c0|1|2\\u003e}` with port→id mapping `8000→0`, `8001→1`, `8002→2`. This is the foundation every later API/realtime/SPA task mounts onto. No remote services and no Docker-in-Docker. Auth, workspaces, SPA, IRC, WebSocket fan-out, and durable schemas are out of scope here; this task only creates process supervision, shared config conventions, and a stub API router that subsequent tasks extend.\\n\\n## Technical Approach\\n\\n**Decision:** One Python 3.12 process image, three OS processes, FastAPI + Uvicorn, supervised by bash `start.sh`, with local `redis-server` as a fourth supervised child.\\n\\n**Why this shape**\\n- The product requires three distinct HTTP listeners and node-kill resilience; three OS processes (not one multi-bind process, not threads-only) match the grader’s SIGKILL model and the 60s auto-restart requirement.\\n- FastAPI gives a single mountable `APIRouter` tree under `/api` so MARATHON-3+ can add modules without rewriting the HTTP entrypoint.\\n- Redis is started now even though health does not use it, because later fan-out and the Redis-outage tests assume the daemon is owned by `start.sh`.\\n- Language/framework are unspecified by the product instruction; Python 3.12 is present in the harness and fits REST + later WebSocket work.\\n\\n**Alternatives considered**\\n1. **Node.js `http` / Express** — viable (Node is installed), but the rest of the epic’s backend tasks are naturally one shared Python package with SQLite; splitting runtimes would complicate shared state.\\n2. **Python stdlib only** — avoids deps, but forces every later task to reinvent routing, JSON validation, multipart, and WebSockets; rejected for velocity and consistency.\\n3. **One process binding all three ports** — fails the “kill one HTTP node” resilience model; rejected.\\n4. **Docker Compose / containers** — prohibited (no DinD / no remote orchestration).\\n\\n**Trade-off accepted:** `start.sh` must ensure FastAPI/Uvicorn (and `redis` client later) are available via a local venv + pinned `requirements.txt`, preferably with wheels under `vendor/` so boot does not depend on live PyPI. Cold start is slightly heavier than stdlib, but the router seam for sibling tasks is far cleaner.\\n\\n**Conventions this design locks for the epic (no sibling designs exist yet)**\\n- Product name: **Huddle**; repo root is `/app` at runtime.\\n- HTTP bind: `127.0.0.1` only; ports `8000/8001/8002`.\\n- `node_id` ∈ `{0,1,2}` derived solely from port (never from startup order).\\n- API prefix: `/api/...`; JSON only for API responses.\\n- Primary durable store (for later tasks): SQLite file at `/app/data/huddle.db` shared by all nodes via the local filesystem. Redis is **not** the source of truth for users/messages — it is for pub/sub fan-out (and optional caches) in later tasks.\\n- Env contract for each node process:\\n  - `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` — integer `0|1|2`\\n  - `HUDDLE_PORT` — integer `8000|8001|8002`\\n  - `HUDDLE_HOST` — `127.0.0.1`\\n  - `HUDDLE_REDIS_URL` — `redis://127.0.0.1:6379/0`\\n  - `HUDDLE_DATA_DIR` — `/app/data` (or `$APP_ROOT/data`)\\n- Error bodies for unspecified errors: FastAPI default `{\\\"detail\\\": ...}` until a later design overrides.\\n- Health **must not** check Redis or SQLite liveness; a live HTTP process alone is enough for `200`.\\n\\n## Interface Contracts\\n\\n### Process / ops seam (verifier ↔ `start.sh`)\\n\\n| Concern | Contract |\\n|---|---|\\n| Entrypoint | Verifier runs `/app/start.sh` (executable). |\\n| Lifetime | `start.sh` stays in the **foreground** until killed; while it is alive the cluster serves. |\\n| Children | Exactly three HTTP node processes + one `redis-server` (plus any short-lived helpers). |\\n| Ports | HTTP: `127.0.0.1:8000`, `:8001`, `:8002`. Redis: `127.0.0.1:6379`. |\\n| Restart | If an HTTP child exits for any reason (including SIGKILL), `start.sh` respawns that node with the same `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`/`HUDDLE_PORT` so it is healthy again **within 60s** (target: a few seconds). If Redis exits, `start.sh` respawns it promptly so pub/sub can resume after later tasks exist. |\\n| Shutdown | On SIGINT/SIGTERM to `start.sh`, terminate children and exit non-zero only on fatal setup failure. |\\n| Isolation | No remote URLs, no Docker, no external managed Redis. |\\n\\n### HTTP seam — `GET /api/health`\\n\\n**Ownership:** Backend only for this task. No frontend consumer yet; when the SPA exists it may probe health but must not block login on it.\\n\\n**Request**\\n- Method/path: `GET /api/health`\\n- Auth: none\\n- Query/body: none\\n\\n**Success response**\\n- Status: `200`\\n- Headers: `Content-Type: application/json`\\n- Body (exact keys; `node_id` is JSON number, not string):\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"status\\\": \\\"ok\\\", \\\"node_id\\\": 0}\\n```\\n\\n| Port | `node_id` |\\n|------|-----------|\\n| 8000 | `0` |\\n| 8001 | `1` |\\n| 8002 | `2` |\\n\\n**Validation**\\n- Server: derive `node_id` from `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` (set by `start.sh` to match the port table). Reject process start if env `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`/`HUDDLE_PORT` disagree with the table.\\n- Client (future SPA / grader): treat non-200 or missing keys as unhealthy; while slow, show no hard dependency in UI for this task.\\n\\n**Failure / slow behavior**\\n- If the node process is down: TCP connection refused / timeout — client retries; `start.sh` is responsible for respawn.\\n- Health handler itself never waits on Redis/SQLite/network; latency should be trivial (ms).\\n- Unknown methods on `/api/health` (e.g. `POST`): `405` or FastAPI default — not required by grader; implementer may leave framework default.\\n\\n### Stub routing seam (this task ↔ later API tasks)\\n\\n- Mount a top-level FastAPI app with `app.include_router(..., prefix=\\\"/api\\\")`.\\n- This task registers only the health router.\\n- Later tasks add sibling modules (e.g. `huddle/api/auth.py`) and `include_router` them from `huddle/api/__init__.py` — **no** second HTTP server.\\n- Unregistered `/api/*` paths return `404 {\\\"detail\\\": ...}` (framework default).\\n- `GET /` is **not** implemented here (SPA is a later task). Returning framework `404` is acceptable until the frontend task adds static hosting.\\n\\n### Cross-node identity seam\\n\\n- All three nodes run the **same code** and same DB file path; only `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` / `HUDDLE_PORT` differ.\\n- Resource ids created in later tasks must be stable cluster-wide (SQLite shared file supports this). This task does not mint resource ids.\\n\\n## Data Model\\n\\nNo application schema in this task.\\n\\n| Store | Role now | Later |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `/app/data/` directory | Created/owned by `start.sh` or first boot | SQLite `huddle.db`, uploads, redis working dir |\\n| Redis @ `127.0.0.1:6379` | Process up; unused by health | Pub/sub fan-out; must survive kill+respawn |\\n| In-process | `node_id`, port from env | Connection pools, WS subscribers |\\n\\n**Schema changes:** none. Backward compatible by vacuity (empty repo → first schema arrives in MARATHON-3+).\\n\\n**Redis config expectations (no ACL/password):**\\n- `bind 127.0.0.1`\\n- `port 6379`\\n- `dir` under `/app/data/redis` (or equivalent local path)\\n- protected-mode as needed for local-only bind\\n- not daemonized by Redis itself — `start.sh` is the supervisor (`--daemonize no`)\\n\\n## Files to Create / Files to Modify\\n\\n### Create\\n\\n| File | Purpose |\\n|---|---|\\n| `start.sh` | Executable foreground supervisor: deps/venv, Redis, three nodes, restart loop, signal handling. |\\n| `requirements.txt` | Pin `fastapi`, `uvicorn[standard]` (and leave room for `redis`, etc. later or add `redis` now unused). Prefer exact pins. |\\n| `vendor/` (optional but recommended) | Bundled wheels so `pip install --no-index` works offline. |\\n| `huddle/__init__.py` | Package marker; version string optional. |\\n| `huddle/config.py` | Read/validate `HUDDLE_*` env; expose `settings.node_id`, `settings.port`, `settings.host`, `settings.redis_url`, `settings.data_dir`. |\\n| `huddle/app.py` | `create_app()` → FastAPI instance; include API router; no business routes beyond what’s included. |\\n| `huddle/api/__init__.py` | Aggregate `api_router`; include health; comment/marker for sibling routers. |\\n| `huddle/api/health.py` | `GET /health` → `{status, node_id}` (mounted under `/api`). |\\n| `huddle/__main__.py` | `uvicorn` entry: `python -m huddle` reads settings and serves `huddle.app:create_app`. |\\n\\n### Modify\\n\\n| File | Change |\\n|---|---|\\n| `.gitignore` | Ignore `.venv/`, `__pycache__/`, `data/*` runtime artifacts (keep `data/.gitkeep` if added), `.timer_start`; do **not** ignore `start.sh` or `huddle/`. |\\n| (optional) `data/.gitkeep` | Ensure `/app/data` exists in the tree. |\\n\\n### Do not modify\\n\\n| File | Reason |\\n|---|---|\\n| `timer.sh` | Harness timer; unrelated. |\\n| `loom-prompts/` | Agent prompts; not product. |\\n\\n## Sequencing and Cross-Task Seams\\n\\n**This task is the root dependency.** MARATHON-3 (auth), MARATHON-4 (profiles), MARATHON-5 (workspaces), and all later backend tasks `depends_on` this scaffold.\\n\\n**Build order inside this task**\\n1. Package layout + `config` + `health` + `create_app`\\n2. `requirements.txt` (+ vendor if used)\\n3. `start.sh` supervision last (needs a runnable module)\\n\\n**Parallelism**\\n- Nothing else in the epic can meaningfully implement against live ports until this lands.\\n- Design work for MARATHON-3+ can proceed in parallel against the contracts below.\\n\\n**Seams owned here that dependents rely on**\\n\\n| Seam | Owner | Consumers |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `start.sh` lifecycle + port map + restart | MARATHON-2 | All runtime tasks, QA, grader |\\n| `GET /api/health` shape | MARATHON-2 | Grader; ops checks |\\n| `huddle.api` router aggregation | MARATHON-2 | MARATHON-3+ route modules |\\n| `HUDDLE_*` env + `/app/data` + Redis URL | MARATHON-2 | Auth persistence, files, WS fan-out |\\n| SQLite path convention `/app/data/huddle.db` | MARATHON-2 (convention only; no schema) | MARATHON-3+ |\\n\\n**Explicitly not split:** do not defer HTTP auto-restart to a later “resilience” task — the product grades restart via `start.sh`, and rewriting supervision later is wasteful. Full Redis-outage **fan-out fallback** logic stays in a future realtime task; this task only respawns Redis.\\n\\n## Out of Scope (Needs a Separate Task)\\n\\nNeighborhood scan of the repo found only `timer.sh`, empty `data/`, and agent prompt/gitignore files — no existing HTTP/Redis/SPA code. From the product instruction / epic, the following are **intentionally excluded** from MARATHON-2:\\n\\n| Item | Why excluded |\\n|---|---|\\n| `GET /` SPA / static assets / `data-testid` hooks | Frontend tasks; not in this task description |\\n| Auth register/login/me (MARATHON-3) | Separate task; needs DB schema |\\n| Profiles, workspaces, channels, messages, files, search, etc. | Later epic children |\\n| WebSocket `/api/ws` + dense `seq` + cross-node fan-out | Realtime task(s); needs Redis pub/sub usage + fallback |\\n| IRC gateway `:6667` | Separate protocol task |\\n| Redis-outage application-level fan-out fallback (direct peer push) | Requires message pipeline; only process respawn is in scope here |\\n| Dependency on Slack SDK / remote APIs | Prohibited entirely |\\n\\nIf the neighborhood later gains half-built servers on these ports, implementers must replace/integrate with this layout rather than run a second cluster.\\n\\n## Edge Cases \\u0026 Error Handling\\n\\n| Situation | Expected behavior |\\n|---|---|\\n| Port 8000/8001/8002 already bound at start | Fail fast with a clear stderr message; exit non-zero. Do not silently bind another port. (QA uses `marathon-freeports` / `marathon-portlock`.) |\\n| Port 6379 already bound by an alien process | Fail fast if it is not our Redis; if a previous Huddle Redis is healthy, either adopt it or kill-and-replace — **prefer**: if `PING` works on `127.0.0.1:6379`, reuse; otherwise abort with error. |\\n| `redis-server` binary missing | Exit non-zero with message; do not pretend health is enough. |\\n| HTTP child SIGKILL’d | Supervisor restarts that node only; other nodes keep serving without interruption. |\\n| Redis SIGKILL’d | Supervisor restarts Redis; HTTP nodes keep answering `/api/health` (and later writes once those exist). Do **not** crash nodes when Redis drops. |\\n| `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` missing/invalid | Node process exits immediately with error; supervisor may restart — ensure `start.sh` always exports valid env so this is a developer mistake, not steady state. |\\n| `start.sh` killed (SIGTERM/SIGINT) | Tear down children; do not leave orphaned redis/node processes. |\\n| `start.sh` killed (SIGKILL) | Best-effort only; orphans possible — acceptable for grader stop. |\\n| Duplicate start of `start.sh` | Second instance should fail on bind; do not require a pidfile unless implementer wants one under `/app/data`. |\\n| Health called during Redis outage | Still `200 {\\\"status\\\":\\\"ok\\\",\\\"node_id\\\":N}`. |\\n| Extra JSON fields on health | Avoid; stick to exact two fields so graders using exact match do not flake. |\\n| `status` value other than `\\\"ok\\\"` | Not used on success path. |\\n| Pip/venv install failure | Exit non-zero before binding ports; log the pip error. |\\n| Working directory | `start.sh` `cd`s to its own directory (`APP_ROOT`) so paths work regardless of caller cwd. |\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. `test -x /app/start.sh` (or `./start.sh` in repo root) succeeds.\\n2. After starting `./start.sh` (under `marathon-portlock` when ports might be contested) and waiting for readiness:\\n   - `curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health` → HTTP 200, JSON `status==\\\"ok\\\"`, `node_id==0`\\n   - same for `:8001` → `node_id==1`\\n   - same for `:8002` → `node_id==2`\\n3. `redis-cli -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6379 PING` → `PONG`.\\n4. `start.sh` remains in the foreground (does not daemonize itself and return immediately).\\n5. `node_id` values are JSON numbers; response contains no requirement for other keys.\\n6. Killing one node’s PID (SIGKILL) does **not** take down the other two health endpoints; within 60s the killed port serves health again with the **same** `node_id`.\\n7. Killing `redis-server` does **not** cause health endpoints to fail; Redis accepts `PING` again after `start.sh` respawns it.\\n8. No Docker socket usage; no calls to external network services required for health.\\n9. A clear module path exists for adding routes (e.g. empty aggregation point in `huddle/api/__init__.py`) so MARATHON-3 can attach `/api/auth/*` without changing `start.sh`.\\n10. Negative: binding must **not** use `0.0.0.0` for the three HTTP nodes (must be `127.0.0.1`).\\n11. Negative: `/api/health` must **not** return `5xx` solely because Redis is down.\\n12. Negative: `node_id` for port 8000 must **not** be `1` or `2` under any restart ordering.\\n\\n## Testing Strategy\\n\\n**Automated / implementer**\\n- Unit: config mapping port↔`node_id`; reject mismatched env.\\n- Unit: health handler returns exact payload for each injected `node_id`.\\n- Integration (script or pytest): boot via `start.sh` (or invoke three modules + redis in a test harness), assert three health payloads + Redis PING.\\n- Chaos smoke: SIGKILL one uvicorn PID and Redis PID; assert criteria 6–7.\\n- Always free ports first: `marathon-freeports`; wrap long-lived servers with `marathon-portlock`. Kill all started processes before finishing.\\n\\n**Human / QA verification**\\n```bash\\nmarathon-freeports\\nmarathon-portlock /app/start.sh   # or ./start.sh from repo root\\n# in another shell:\\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health\\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/health\\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8002/api/health\\nredis-cli -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6379 PING\\n# SIGKILL one node PID; re-curl within 60s\\n# SIGKILL redis; confirm health still 200; wait for PING\\n```\\n\\n**Out of test scope for this task:** auth flows, SPA, IRC, WebSocket seq denseness, Redis fan-out fallback semantics beyond process respawn.\",\n  \"design_format\": \"markdown\",\n  \"has_design\": true,\n  \"notes\": \"New design (no needs-revision). Greenfield repo; no sibling designs. Locked stack: Python3+FastAPI+Uvicorn, start.sh supervises 3 nodes+Redis with restart, health independent of Redis, shared data dir /app/data (SQLite path convention for dependents). Handing to review; implementer label remains backend after architect approval.\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-08-22T18:36:36.074640642Z\",\n  \"updated_at\": \"2026-08-22T18:41:30.536865937Z\",\n  \"created_by\": \"marathon-harness\",\n  \"description\": \"Add /app/start.sh that stays in the foreground and keeps the cluster serving while alive. Launch three HTTP nodes on 127.0.0.1:8000, :8001, :8002 and a local redis-server on 127.0.0.1:6379. Implement GET /api/health returning 200 {status:ok, node_id} with node_id 0/1/2 matching ports. Self-contained only — no remote services, no Docker-in-Docker. Stub routing so other tasks can attach APIs.\",\n  \"dependents\": [\n    {\n      \"issue_id\": \"MARATHON-3\",\n      \"depends_on_id\": \"MARATHON-2\",\n      \"type\": \"blocks\",\n      \"title\": \"Auth: register, login, me, bearer tokens\",\n      \"status\": \"open\",\n      \"issue_type\": \"task\",\n      \"created_at\": \"2026-08-22T18:36:49.095074015Z\",\n      \"created_by\": \"marathon-harness\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"issue_id\": \"MARATHON-4\",\n      \"depends_on_id\": \"MARATHON-2\",\n      \"type\": \"blocks\",\n      \"title\": \"User profiles: GET user and PATCH me\",\n      \"status\": \"open\",\n      \"priority\": 1,\n      \"issue_type\": \"task\",\n      \"created_at\": \"2026-08-22T18:36:49.120686578Z\",\n      \"created_by\": \"marathon-harness\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"issue_id\": \"MARATHON-5\",\n      \"depends_on_id\": \"MARATHON-2\",\n      \"type\": \"blocks\",\n      \"title\": \"Workspaces: create, list, get with #general\",\n      \"status\": \"open\",\n      \"issue_type\": \"task\",\n      \"created_at\": \"2026-08-22T18:36:49.139Z\",\n      \"created_by\": \"marathon-harness\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"issue_id\": \"MARATHON-21\",\n      \"depends_on_id\": \"MARATHON-2\",\n      \"type\": \"blocks\",\n      \"title\": \"HTTP node auto-restart within 60s\",\n      \"status\": \"open\",\n      \"issue_type\": \"task\",\n      \"created_at\": \"2026-08-22T18:37:57.518592671Z\",\n      \"created_by\": \"marathon-harness\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\nThe 'loom data' subtree contains thin CLI commands that interact\nwith the configured loom issue backend. When --server or LOOM_SERVER_URL is\nset, commands talk to that loom server over HTTP. Without a server, issue\ncommands use the local backend selected by the workspace configuration and\ndaemon IPC environment.\n\nUse 'loom data' commands when:\n  • You want a backend-aware command surface for agents and scripts.\n  • You want to manage agents on a remote loom server.\n  • You are scripting against a hosted loom deployment.\n\nExamples:\n  loom data show <id> --server http://localhost:8080\n  loom data ready --limit 10\n  loom data list --server http://localhost:8080 -o json | jq '.[].id'\n  loom data monitor --server http://localhost:8080\n  loom data agent stop falcon --server http://localhost:8080\n\nRoot-level --server / --workspace are mirrored into LOOM_SERVER_URL /\nLOOM_WORKSPACE in PersistentPreRunE, so 'loom --server URL data show ID'\nand 'loom data show ID --server URL' resolve identically. The data\nsubcommand reads from env, not from its own flag.\n\nUsage:\n  loom data [command]\n\nAvailable Commands:\n  agent       Control a single agent on the loom server (stop/start/restart/yield)\n  agents      List agents managed by the loom server daemon (HTTP)\n  blocked     List issues that are blocked by other open issues (HTTP)\n  claim       Atomically claim an issue\n  close       Close an issue (HTTP)\n  comment     Add a comment to an issue (HTTP)\n  create      Create an issue\n  list        List issues (HTTP)\n  monitor     Show monitor dashboard from a loom server (HTTP, single-shot)\n  ready       List issues that are ready to work on (HTTP)\n  show        Show a single issue by ID (HTTP)\n  update      Update issue fields\n\nFlags:\n  -h, --help            help for data\n  -o, --output string   Output format: text|json (default \"text\")\n      --server string   Loom server base URL (or LOOM_SERVER_URL env var)\n\nGlobal Flags:\n      --backend string      AI backend CLI to use (codex, claude, opencode). Env: LOOM_BACKEND\n      --log-format string   Log format (text|json) (default \"text\")\n      --log-output string   Log output destination (stderr|<filepath>) (default \"stderr\")\n      --workspace string    Workspace ID. Env: LOOM_WORKSPACE\n\nUse \"loom data [command] --help\" for more information about a command.\n","stderr":"time=2026-08-22T18:45:23.166Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:35473\n","executionTime":390,"interleavedOutput":"Already up to date.\ntime=2026-08-22T18:45:23.166Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:35473\n{\n  \"id\": \"MARATHON-2\",\n  \"title\": \"Cluster scaffold: start.sh, 3 HTTP nodes, Redis, health\",\n  \"status\": \"in_progress\",\n  \"priority\": 0,\n  \"issue_type\": \"task\",\n  \"assignee\": \"frontend-dev-1\",\n  \"owner\": \"marathon-harness\",\n  \"labels\": [\n    \"backend\"\n  ],\n  \"source_repo\": \"app\",\n  \"parent\": \"MARATHON-1\",\n  \"design\": \"## Summary\\n\\nDeliver a bootable Huddle cluster skeleton at `/app`: a foreground `/app/start.sh` that launches local Redis on `127.0.0.1:6379` and three identical HTTP nodes on `127.0.0.1:8000/8001/8002`, plus `GET /api/health` returning `200 {\\\"status\\\":\\\"ok\\\",\\\"node_id\\\":\\u003c0|1|2\\u003e}` with port→id mapping `8000→0`, `8001→1`, `8002→2`. This is the foundation every later API/realtime/SPA task mounts onto. No remote services and no Docker-in-Docker. Auth, workspaces, SPA, IRC, WebSocket fan-out, and durable schemas are out of scope here; this task only creates process supervision, shared config conventions, and a stub API router that subsequent tasks extend.\\n\\n## Technical Approach\\n\\n**Decision:** One Python 3.12 process image, three OS processes, FastAPI + Uvicorn, supervised by bash `start.sh`, with local `redis-server` as a fourth supervised child.\\n\\n**Why this shape**\\n- The product requires three distinct HTTP listeners and node-kill resilience; three OS processes (not one multi-bind process, not threads-only) match the grader’s SIGKILL model and the 60s auto-restart requirement.\\n- FastAPI gives a single mountable `APIRouter` tree under `/api` so MARATHON-3+ can add modules without rewriting the HTTP entrypoint.\\n- Redis is started now even though health does not use it, because later fan-out and the Redis-outage tests assume the daemon is owned by `start.sh`.\\n- Language/framework are unspecified by the product instruction; Python 3.12 is present in the harness and fits REST + later WebSocket work.\\n\\n**Alternatives considered**\\n1. **Node.js `http` / Express** — viable (Node is installed), but the rest of the epic’s backend tasks are naturally one shared Python package with SQLite; splitting runtimes would complicate shared state.\\n2. **Python stdlib only** — avoids deps, but forces every later task to reinvent routing, JSON validation, multipart, and WebSockets; rejected for velocity and consistency.\\n3. **One process binding all three ports** — fails the “kill one HTTP node” resilience model; rejected.\\n4. **Docker Compose / containers** — prohibited (no DinD / no remote orchestration).\\n\\n**Trade-off accepted:** `start.sh` must ensure FastAPI/Uvicorn (and `redis` client later) are available via a local venv + pinned `requirements.txt`, preferably with wheels under `vendor/` so boot does not depend on live PyPI. Cold start is slightly heavier than stdlib, but the router seam for sibling tasks is far cleaner.\\n\\n**Conventions this design locks for the epic (no sibling designs exist yet)**\\n- Product name: **Huddle**; repo root is `/app` at runtime.\\n- HTTP bind: `127.0.0.1` only; ports `8000/8001/8002`.\\n- `node_id` ∈ `{0,1,2}` derived solely from port (never from startup order).\\n- API prefix: `/api/...`; JSON only for API responses.\\n- Primary durable store (for later tasks): SQLite file at `/app/data/huddle.db` shared by all nodes via the local filesystem. Redis is **not** the source of truth for users/messages — it is for pub/sub fan-out (and optional caches) in later tasks.\\n- Env contract for each node process:\\n  - `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` — integer `0|1|2`\\n  - `HUDDLE_PORT` — integer `8000|8001|8002`\\n  - `HUDDLE_HOST` — `127.0.0.1`\\n  - `HUDDLE_REDIS_URL` — `redis://127.0.0.1:6379/0`\\n  - `HUDDLE_DATA_DIR` — `/app/data` (or `$APP_ROOT/data`)\\n- Error bodies for unspecified errors: FastAPI default `{\\\"detail\\\": ...}` until a later design overrides.\\n- Health **must not** check Redis or SQLite liveness; a live HTTP process alone is enough for `200`.\\n\\n## Interface Contracts\\n\\n### Process / ops seam (verifier ↔ `start.sh`)\\n\\n| Concern | Contract |\\n|---|---|\\n| Entrypoint | Verifier runs `/app/start.sh` (executable). |\\n| Lifetime | `start.sh` stays in the **foreground** until killed; while it is alive the cluster serves. |\\n| Children | Exactly three HTTP node processes + one `redis-server` (plus any short-lived helpers). |\\n| Ports | HTTP: `127.0.0.1:8000`, `:8001`, `:8002`. Redis: `127.0.0.1:6379`. |\\n| Restart | If an HTTP child exits for any reason (including SIGKILL), `start.sh` respawns that node with the same `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`/`HUDDLE_PORT` so it is healthy again **within 60s** (target: a few seconds). If Redis exits, `start.sh` respawns it promptly so pub/sub can resume after later tasks exist. |\\n| Shutdown | On SIGINT/SIGTERM to `start.sh`, terminate children and exit non-zero only on fatal setup failure. |\\n| Isolation | No remote URLs, no Docker, no external managed Redis. |\\n\\n### HTTP seam — `GET /api/health`\\n\\n**Ownership:** Backend only for this task. No frontend consumer yet; when the SPA exists it may probe health but must not block login on it.\\n\\n**Request**\\n- Method/path: `GET /api/health`\\n- Auth: none\\n- Query/body: none\\n\\n**Success response**\\n- Status: `200`\\n- Headers: `Content-Type: application/json`\\n- Body (exact keys; `node_id` is JSON number, not string):\\n\\n```json\\n{\\\"status\\\": \\\"ok\\\", \\\"node_id\\\": 0}\\n```\\n\\n| Port | `node_id` |\\n|------|-----------|\\n| 8000 | `0` |\\n| 8001 | `1` |\\n| 8002 | `2` |\\n\\n**Validation**\\n- Server: derive `node_id` from `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` (set by `start.sh` to match the port table). Reject process start if env `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`/`HUDDLE_PORT` disagree with the table.\\n- Client (future SPA / grader): treat non-200 or missing keys as unhealthy; while slow, show no hard dependency in UI for this task.\\n\\n**Failure / slow behavior**\\n- If the node process is down: TCP connection refused / timeout — client retries; `start.sh` is responsible for respawn.\\n- Health handler itself never waits on Redis/SQLite/network; latency should be trivial (ms).\\n- Unknown methods on `/api/health` (e.g. `POST`): `405` or FastAPI default — not required by grader; implementer may leave framework default.\\n\\n### Stub routing seam (this task ↔ later API tasks)\\n\\n- Mount a top-level FastAPI app with `app.include_router(..., prefix=\\\"/api\\\")`.\\n- This task registers only the health router.\\n- Later tasks add sibling modules (e.g. `huddle/api/auth.py`) and `include_router` them from `huddle/api/__init__.py` — **no** second HTTP server.\\n- Unregistered `/api/*` paths return `404 {\\\"detail\\\": ...}` (framework default).\\n- `GET /` is **not** implemented here (SPA is a later task). Returning framework `404` is acceptable until the frontend task adds static hosting.\\n\\n### Cross-node identity seam\\n\\n- All three nodes run the **same code** and same DB file path; only `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` / `HUDDLE_PORT` differ.\\n- Resource ids created in later tasks must be stable cluster-wide (SQLite shared file supports this). This task does not mint resource ids.\\n\\n## Data Model\\n\\nNo application schema in this task.\\n\\n| Store | Role now | Later |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `/app/data/` directory | Created/owned by `start.sh` or first boot | SQLite `huddle.db`, uploads, redis working dir |\\n| Redis @ `127.0.0.1:6379` | Process up; unused by health | Pub/sub fan-out; must survive kill+respawn |\\n| In-process | `node_id`, port from env | Connection pools, WS subscribers |\\n\\n**Schema changes:** none. Backward compatible by vacuity (empty repo → first schema arrives in MARATHON-3+).\\n\\n**Redis config expectations (no ACL/password):**\\n- `bind 127.0.0.1`\\n- `port 6379`\\n- `dir` under `/app/data/redis` (or equivalent local path)\\n- protected-mode as needed for local-only bind\\n- not daemonized by Redis itself — `start.sh` is the supervisor (`--daemonize no`)\\n\\n## Files to Create / Files to Modify\\n\\n### Create\\n\\n| File | Purpose |\\n|---|---|\\n| `start.sh` | Executable foreground supervisor: deps/venv, Redis, three nodes, restart loop, signal handling. |\\n| `requirements.txt` | Pin `fastapi`, `uvicorn[standard]` (and leave room for `redis`, etc. later or add `redis` now unused). Prefer exact pins. |\\n| `vendor/` (optional but recommended) | Bundled wheels so `pip install --no-index` works offline. |\\n| `huddle/__init__.py` | Package marker; version string optional. |\\n| `huddle/config.py` | Read/validate `HUDDLE_*` env; expose `settings.node_id`, `settings.port`, `settings.host`, `settings.redis_url`, `settings.data_dir`. |\\n| `huddle/app.py` | `create_app()` → FastAPI instance; include API router; no business routes beyond what’s included. |\\n| `huddle/api/__init__.py` | Aggregate `api_router`; include health; comment/marker for sibling routers. |\\n| `huddle/api/health.py` | `GET /health` → `{status, node_id}` (mounted under `/api`). |\\n| `huddle/__main__.py` | `uvicorn` entry: `python -m huddle` reads settings and serves `huddle.app:create_app`. |\\n\\n### Modify\\n\\n| File | Change |\\n|---|---|\\n| `.gitignore` | Ignore `.venv/`, `__pycache__/`, `data/*` runtime artifacts (keep `data/.gitkeep` if added), `.timer_start`; do **not** ignore `start.sh` or `huddle/`. |\\n| (optional) `data/.gitkeep` | Ensure `/app/data` exists in the tree. |\\n\\n### Do not modify\\n\\n| File | Reason |\\n|---|---|\\n| `timer.sh` | Harness timer; unrelated. |\\n| `loom-prompts/` | Agent prompts; not product. |\\n\\n## Sequencing and Cross-Task Seams\\n\\n**This task is the root dependency.** MARATHON-3 (auth), MARATHON-4 (profiles), MARATHON-5 (workspaces), and all later backend tasks `depends_on` this scaffold.\\n\\n**Build order inside this task**\\n1. Package layout + `config` + `health` + `create_app`\\n2. `requirements.txt` (+ vendor if used)\\n3. `start.sh` supervision last (needs a runnable module)\\n\\n**Parallelism**\\n- Nothing else in the epic can meaningfully implement against live ports until this lands.\\n- Design work for MARATHON-3+ can proceed in parallel against the contracts below.\\n\\n**Seams owned here that dependents rely on**\\n\\n| Seam | Owner | Consumers |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `start.sh` lifecycle + port map + restart | MARATHON-2 | All runtime tasks, QA, grader |\\n| `GET /api/health` shape | MARATHON-2 | Grader; ops checks |\\n| `huddle.api` router aggregation | MARATHON-2 | MARATHON-3+ route modules |\\n| `HUDDLE_*` env + `/app/data` + Redis URL | MARATHON-2 | Auth persistence, files, WS fan-out |\\n| SQLite path convention `/app/data/huddle.db` | MARATHON-2 (convention only; no schema) | MARATHON-3+ |\\n\\n**Explicitly not split:** do not defer HTTP auto-restart to a later “resilience” task — the product grades restart via `start.sh`, and rewriting supervision later is wasteful. Full Redis-outage **fan-out fallback** logic stays in a future realtime task; this task only respawns Redis.\\n\\n## Out of Scope (Needs a Separate Task)\\n\\nNeighborhood scan of the repo found only `timer.sh`, empty `data/`, and agent prompt/gitignore files — no existing HTTP/Redis/SPA code. From the product instruction / epic, the following are **intentionally excluded** from MARATHON-2:\\n\\n| Item | Why excluded |\\n|---|---|\\n| `GET /` SPA / static assets / `data-testid` hooks | Frontend tasks; not in this task description |\\n| Auth register/login/me (MARATHON-3) | Separate task; needs DB schema |\\n| Profiles, workspaces, channels, messages, files, search, etc. | Later epic children |\\n| WebSocket `/api/ws` + dense `seq` + cross-node fan-out | Realtime task(s); needs Redis pub/sub usage + fallback |\\n| IRC gateway `:6667` | Separate protocol task |\\n| Redis-outage application-level fan-out fallback (direct peer push) | Requires message pipeline; only process respawn is in scope here |\\n| Dependency on Slack SDK / remote APIs | Prohibited entirely |\\n\\nIf the neighborhood later gains half-built servers on these ports, implementers must replace/integrate with this layout rather than run a second cluster.\\n\\n## Edge Cases \\u0026 Error Handling\\n\\n| Situation | Expected behavior |\\n|---|---|\\n| Port 8000/8001/8002 already bound at start | Fail fast with a clear stderr message; exit non-zero. Do not silently bind another port. (QA uses `marathon-freeports` / `marathon-portlock`.) |\\n| Port 6379 already bound by an alien process | Fail fast if it is not our Redis; if a previous Huddle Redis is healthy, either adopt it or kill-and-replace — **prefer**: if `PING` works on `127.0.0.1:6379`, reuse; otherwise abort with error. |\\n| `redis-server` binary missing | Exit non-zero with message; do not pretend health is enough. |\\n| HTTP child SIGKILL’d | Supervisor restarts that node only; other nodes keep serving without interruption. |\\n| Redis SIGKILL’d | Supervisor restarts Redis; HTTP nodes keep answering `/api/health` (and later writes once those exist). Do **not** crash nodes when Redis drops. |\\n| `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` missing/invalid | Node process exits immediately with error; supervisor may restart — ensure `start.sh` always exports valid env so this is a developer mistake, not steady state. |\\n| `start.sh` killed (SIGTERM/SIGINT) | Tear down children; do not leave orphaned redis/node processes. |\\n| `start.sh` killed (SIGKILL) | Best-effort only; orphans possible — acceptable for grader stop. |\\n| Duplicate start of `start.sh` | Second instance should fail on bind; do not require a pidfile unless implementer wants one under `/app/data`. |\\n| Health called during Redis outage | Still `200 {\\\"status\\\":\\\"ok\\\",\\\"node_id\\\":N}`. |\\n| Extra JSON fields on health | Avoid; stick to exact two fields so graders using exact match do not flake. |\\n| `status` value other than `\\\"ok\\\"` | Not used on success path. |\\n| Pip/venv install failure | Exit non-zero before binding ports; log the pip error. |\\n| Working directory | `start.sh` `cd`s to its own directory (`APP_ROOT`) so paths work regardless of caller cwd. |\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. `test -x /app/start.sh` (or `./start.sh` in repo root) succeeds.\\n2. After starting `./start.sh` (under `marathon-portlock` when ports might be contested) and waiting for readiness:\\n   - `curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health` → HTTP 200, JSON `status==\\\"ok\\\"`, `node_id==0`\\n   - same for `:8001` → `node_id==1`\\n   - same for `:8002` → `node_id==2`\\n3. `redis-cli -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6379 PING` → `PONG`.\\n4. `start.sh` remains in the foreground (does not daemonize itself and return immediately).\\n5. `node_id` values are JSON numbers; response contains no requirement for other keys.\\n6. Killing one node’s PID (SIGKILL) does **not** take down the other two health endpoints; within 60s the killed port serves health again with the **same** `node_id`.\\n7. Killing `redis-server` does **not** cause health endpoints to fail; Redis accepts `PING` again after `start.sh` respawns it.\\n8. No Docker socket usage; no calls to external network services required for health.\\n9. A clear module path exists for adding routes (e.g. empty aggregation point in `huddle/api/__init__.py`) so MARATHON-3 can attach `/api/auth/*` without changing `start.sh`.\\n10. Negative: binding must **not** use `0.0.0.0` for the three HTTP nodes (must be `127.0.0.1`).\\n11. Negative: `/api/health` must **not** return `5xx` solely because Redis is down.\\n12. Negative: `node_id` for port 8000 must **not** be `1` or `2` under any restart ordering.\\n\\n## Testing Strategy\\n\\n**Automated / implementer**\\n- Unit: config mapping port↔`node_id`; reject mismatched env.\\n- Unit: health handler returns exact payload for each injected `node_id`.\\n- Integration (script or pytest): boot via `start.sh` (or invoke three modules + redis in a test harness), assert three health payloads + Redis PING.\\n- Chaos smoke: SIGKILL one uvicorn PID and Redis PID; assert criteria 6–7.\\n- Always free ports first: `marathon-freeports`; wrap long-lived servers with `marathon-portlock`. Kill all started processes before finishing.\\n\\n**Human / QA verification**\\n```bash\\nmarathon-freeports\\nmarathon-portlock /app/start.sh   # or ./start.sh from repo root\\n# in another shell:\\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health\\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/health\\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8002/api/health\\nredis-cli -h 127.0.0.1 -p 6379 PING\\n# SIGKILL one node PID; re-curl within 60s\\n# SIGKILL redis; confirm health still 200; wait for PING\\n```\\n\\n**Out of test scope for this task:** auth flows, SPA, IRC, WebSocket seq denseness, Redis fan-out fallback semantics beyond process respawn.\",\n  \"design_format\": \"markdown\",\n  \"has_design\": true,\n  \"notes\": \"New design (no needs-revision). Greenfield repo; no sibling designs. Locked stack: Python3+FastAPI+Uvicorn, start.sh supervises 3 nodes+Redis with restart, health independent of Redis, shared data dir /app/data (SQLite path convention for dependents). Handing to review; implementer label remains backend after architect approval.\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-08-22T18:36:36.074640642Z\",\n  \"updated_at\": \"2026-08-22T18:41:30.536865937Z\",\n  \"created_by\": \"marathon-harness\",\n  \"description\": \"Add /app/start.sh that stays in the foreground and keeps the cluster serving while alive. Launch three HTTP nodes on 127.0.0.1:8000, :8001, :8002 and a local redis-server on 127.0.0.1:6379. Implement GET /api/health returning 200 {status:ok, node_id} with node_id 0/1/2 matching ports. Self-contained only — no remote services, no Docker-in-Docker. Stub routing so other tasks can attach APIs.\",\n  \"dependents\": [\n    {\n      \"issue_id\": \"MARATHON-3\",\n      \"depends_on_id\": \"MARATHON-2\",\n      \"type\": \"blocks\",\n      \"title\": \"Auth: register, login, me, bearer tokens\",\n      \"status\": \"open\",\n      \"issue_type\": \"task\",\n      \"created_at\": \"2026-08-22T18:36:49.095074015Z\",\n      \"created_by\": \"marathon-harness\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"issue_id\": \"MARATHON-4\",\n      \"depends_on_id\": \"MARATHON-2\",\n      \"type\": \"blocks\",\n      \"title\": \"User profiles: GET user and PATCH me\",\n      \"status\": \"open\",\n      \"priority\": 1,\n      \"issue_type\": \"task\",\n      \"created_at\": \"2026-08-22T18:36:49.120686578Z\",\n      \"created_by\": \"marathon-harness\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"issue_id\": \"MARATHON-5\",\n      \"depends_on_id\": \"MARATHON-2\",\n      \"type\": \"blocks\",\n      \"title\": \"Workspaces: create, list, get with #general\",\n      \"status\": \"open\",\n      \"issue_type\": \"task\",\n      \"created_at\": \"2026-08-22T18:36:49.139Z\",\n      \"created_by\": \"marathon-harness\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"issue_id\": \"MARATHON-21\",\n      \"depends_on_id\": \"MARATHON-2\",\n      \"type\": \"blocks\",\n      \"title\": \"HTTP node auto-restart within 60s\",\n      \"status\": \"open\",\n      \"issue_type\": \"task\",\n      \"created_at\": \"2026-08-22T18:37:57.518592671Z\",\n      \"created_by\": \"marathon-harness\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\nThe 'loom data' subtree contains thin CLI commands that interact\nwith the configured loom issue backend. When --server or LOOM_SERVER_URL is\nset, commands talk to that loom server over HTTP. Without a server, issue\ncommands use the local backend selected by the workspace configuration and\ndaemon IPC environment.\n\nUse 'loom data' commands when:\n  • You want a backend-aware command surface for agents and scripts.\n  • You want to manage agents on a remote loom server.\n  • You are scripting against a hosted loom deployment.\n\nExamples:\n  loom data show <id> --server http://localhost:8080\n  loom data ready --limit 10\n  loom data list --server http://localhost:8080 -o json | jq '.[].id'\n  loom data monitor --server http://localhost:8080\n  loom data agent stop falcon --server http://localhost:8080\n\nRoot-level --server / --workspace are mirrored into LOOM_SERVER_URL /\nLOOM_WORKSPACE in PersistentPreRunE, so 'loom --server URL data show ID'\nand 'loom data show ID --server URL' resolve identically. The data\nsubcommand reads from env, not from its own flag.\n\nUsage:\n  loom data [command]\n\nAvailable Commands:\n  agent       Control a single agent on the loom server (stop/start/restart/yield)\n  agents      List agents managed by the loom server daemon (HTTP)\n  blocked     List issues that are blocked by other open issues (HTTP)\n  claim       Atomically claim an issue\n  close       Close an issue (HTTP)\n  comment     Add a comment to an issue (HTTP)\n  create      Create an issue\n  list        List issues (HTTP)\n  monitor     Show monitor dashboard from a loom server (HTTP, single-shot)\n  ready       List issues that are ready to work on (HTTP)\n  show        Show a single issue by ID (HTTP)\n  update      Update issue fields\n\nFlags:\n  -h, --help            help for data\n  -o, --output string   Output format: text|json (default \"text\")\n      --server string   Loom server base URL (or LOOM_SERVER_URL env var)\n\nGlobal Flags:\n      --backend string      AI backend CLI to use (codex, claude, opencode). Env: LOOM_BACKEND\n      --log-format string   Log format (text|json) (default \"text\")\n      --log-output string   Log output destination (stderr|<filepath>) (default \"stderr\")\n      --workspace string    Workspace ID. Env: LOOM_WORKSPACE\n\nUse \"loom data [command] --help\" for more information about a command.\n","localExecutionTimeMs":67},"isBackground":false},"description":"Merge main and show task details"},"hookAdditionalContexts":[],"toolCallId":"call-9f141dca-5940-45f5-aad9-96f69f1e406f-0\nfc_7f8049a5-599a-97e0-a25d-7da3d4943cb1_0","startedAtMs":"1787424322824","completedAtMs":"1787424323214"},"model_call_id":"507115f0-fb9b-4c24-8b3e-645abf4e560c-0-1r9m","session_id":"15abacc6-9531-4e57-8c31-24c1bdbac2cf","timestamp_ms":1787424323248}
