tend-dev-1 Labels: backend  Description: Add /app/start.sh that stays in the foreground and keeps the cluster serving while alive. Launch three HTTP nodes on 127.0.0.1:8000, :8001, :8002 and a local redis-server on 127.0.0.1:6379. Implement GET /api/health returning 200 {status:ok, node_id} with node_id 0/1/2 matching ports. Self-contained only ??? no remote services, no Docker-in-Docker. Stub routing so other tasks can attach APIs.  Design: ## Summary  Deliver a bootable Huddle cluster skeleton at `/app`: a foreground `/app/start.sh` that launches local Redis on `127.0.0.1:6379` and three identical HTTP nodes on `127.0.0.1:8000/8001/8002`, plus `GET /api/health` returning `200 {\"status\":\"ok\",\"node_id\":<0|1|2>}` with port???id mapping `8000???0`, `8001???1`, `8002???2`. This is the foundation every later API/realtime/SPA task mounts onto. No remote services and no Docker-in-Docker. Auth, workspaces, SPA, IRC, WebSocket fan-out, and durable schemas are out of scope here; this task only creates process supervision, shared config conventions, and a stub API router that subsequent tasks extend.  ## Technical Approach  **Decision:** One Python 3.12 process image, three OS processes, FastAPI + Uvicorn, supervised by bash `start.sh`, with local `redis-server` as a fourth supervised child.  **Why this shape** - The product requires three distinct HTTP listeners and node-kill resilience; three OS processes (not one multi-bind process, not threads-only) match the grader???s SIGKILL model and the 60s auto-restart requirement. - FastAPI gives a single mountable `APIRouter` tree under `/api` so MARATHON-3+ can add modules without rewriting the HTTP entrypoint. - Redis is started now even though health does not use it, because later fan-out and the Redis-outage tests assume the daemon is owned by `start.sh`. - Language/framework are unspecified by the product instruction; Python 3.12 is present in the harness and fits REST + later WebSocket work.  **Alternatives considered*