kend  Description: Add /app/start.sh that stays in the foreground and keeps the cluster serving while alive. Launch three HTTP nodes on 127.0.0.1:8000, :8001, :8002 and a local redis-server on 127.0.0.1:6379. Implement GET /api/health returning 200 {status:ok, node_id} with node_id 0/1/2 matching ports. Self-contained only ??? no remote services, no Docker-in-Docker. Stub routing so other tasks can attach APIs.  Design: ## Summary  Deliver a bootable Huddle cluster skeleton at `/app`: a foreground `/app/start.sh` that launches local Redis on `127.0.0.1:6379` and three identical HTTP nodes on `127.0.0.1:8000/8001/8002`, plus `GET /api/health` returning `200 {\"status\":\"ok\",\"node_id\":<0|1|2>}` with port???id mapping `8000???0`, `8001???1`, `8002???2`. This is the foundation every later API/realtime/SPA task mounts onto. No remote services and no Docker-in-Docker. Auth, workspaces, SPA, IRC, WebSocket fan-out, and durable schemas are out of scope here; this task only creates process supervision, shared config conventions, and a stub API router that subsequent tasks extend.  ## Technical Approach  **Decision:** One Python 3.12 process image, three OS processes, FastAPI + Uvicorn, supervised by bash `start.sh`, with local `redis-server` as a fourth supervised child.  **Why this shape** - The product requires three distinct HTTP listeners and node-kill resilience; three OS processes (not one multi-bind process, not threads-only) match the grader???s SIGKILL model and the 60s auto-restart requirement. - FastAPI gives a single mountable `APIRouter` tree under `/api` so MARATHON-3+ can add modules without rewriting the HTTP entrypoint. - Redis is started now even though health does not use it, because later fan-out and the Redis-outage tests assume the daemon is owned by `start.sh`. - Language/framework are unspecified by the product instruction; Python 3.12 is present in the harness and fits REST + later WebSocket work.  **Alternatives considered** 1. **Node.js `http` / Express** ??? viable (Node is installed), but the rest of the epic???s backend tasks are naturally one shared Python package with SQLite; splitting runtimes would complicate shared state. 2. **Python stdlib only** ??? avoids deps, but forces every later task to reinvent routing, JSON validation, multipart, and WebSockets; rejected for velocity and consistency. 3. **One process binding all three ports** ??? fails the ???kill one HTTP node??? resilience model; rejected. 4. **Docker Compose / containers** ??? prohibited (no DinD / no remote orchestration).  **Trade-off accepted:** `start.sh` must ensure FastAPI/Uvicorn (and `redis` client later) are available via a local venv + pinned `requirements.txt`, preferably with wheels under `vendor/` so boot does not depend on live PyPI. Cold start is slightly heavier than stdlib, but the router seam for sibling tasks is far cleaner.  **Conventions this design locks for the epic (no sibling designs exist yet)** - Product name: **Huddle**; repo root is `/app` at runtime. - HTTP bind: `127.0.0.1` only; ports `8000/8001/8002`. - `node_id` ??? `{0,1,2}` derived solely from port (never from startup order). - API prefix: `/api/...`; JSON only for API responses. - Primary durable store (for later tasks): SQLite file at `/app/data/huddle.db` shared by all nodes via the local filesystem. Redis is **not** the source of truth for users/messages ??? it is for pub/sub fan-out (and optional caches) in later tasks. - Env contract for each node process:   - `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` ??? integer `0|1|2`   - `HUDDLE_PORT` ??? integer `8000|8001|8002`   - `HUDDLE_HOST` ??? `127.0.0.1`   - `HUDDLE_REDIS_URL` ??? `redis://127.0.0.1:6379/0`   - `HUDDLE_DATA_DIR` ??? `/app/data` (or `$APP_ROOT/data`) - Error bodies for unspecified errors: FastAPI default `{\"detail\": ...}` until a later design overrides. - Health **must not** check Redis or SQLite liveness; a live HTTP process alone is enough for `200`.  ## Interface Contracts  ### Process / ops seam (verifier ??? `start.sh`)  | Concern | Contract | |---|---| | Entrypoint | Verifier runs `/app/start.sh` (executable). | | Lifetime | `start.sh` stays in the **foreground** until killed; while it is alive the cluster serves. | | Children | Exactly three HTTP node processes + one `redis-server` (plus any short-lived helpers). | | Ports | HTTP: `127.0.0.1:8000`, `:8001`, `:8002`. Redis: `127.0.0.1:6379`. | | Restart | If an HTTP child ... [truncated]  Notes: New design (no needs-revision). Greenfield repo; no sibling designs. Locked stack: Python3+FastAPI+Uvicorn, start.sh supervises 3 nodes+Redis with restart, health independent of Redis, shared data dir /app/data (SQLite path convention for dependents). Handing to review; implementer label remains backend after architect approval.   ### Step 2: Ground Yourself Before Building  #### 2a. Read the Epic - Determine the parent epic from the task ID or the parent field of 'loom data show <id> --output json' - Run 'loom data show <epic-id>' and read its notes ??? conventions recorded there are **authoritative**  #### 2b. Read Dependencies and Siblings - Check the depends_on field of 'loom data show <id> --output json'. If a dependency is still open, go to Step 6a. - Read two or three closed sibling tasks in the epic for the component names, prop shapes, and styling conventions they established. Match them.  #### 2c. Read the Design and Reconcile Against the Code - Read the design in full: component inventory, states, responsive behavior, accessibility criteria - Open every file the design names and read it as it exists on disk right now - Run 'git log --oneline -5 -- <file>' on each to see whether a sibling task already moved it - Where the code has moved on, follow the design's **intent** and the code's **current** conventions. Where the design is fundamentally unbuildable, go to Step 6b.  #### 2d. Read the Neighborhood Open the components next to the ones you are changing. Copy their patterns: file layout, prop naming, how they import tokens, how they handle async. Consistency here is worth more than your preferred style.  ### Step 3: Build  - Work through the design's component inventory. Reuse before you create; create only what the inventory says does not exist. - Keep every change inside this task's scope. Do not refactor neighbors you happened to read. - Use the existing style tokens and scale steps. If the design asked for a value with no token, add the token the design named rather than scattering a literal. - Wire the real data path. Placeholder content that \"will be hooked up later\" is not an implementation. - **No TODO comments for deferred work.** If something in the design cannot be built yet, record it in task notes so it becomes tracked work ??? a TODO in the code is invisible to the board and never gets done.  ### Step 4: Verify It Renders  Build the project first and fix every compile and type error before going further.  Then exercise the UI, do not read it: - Before starting a command that binds the app's fixed ports, run `marathon-freeports` if a port is busy, then wrap the command as `marathon-portlock <cmd>`. Four workers share the ports and the lock serializes them. - Start the app and open the pages you changed in a real browser - Walk each state the design specifies: **loading**, **empty**, **error**, and the happy path. Force them ??? delay the request, empty the data, break the endpoint. A state you never saw is a state you did not implement. - Check the responsive behavior at each breakpoint the design names - Check the accessibility basics: heading order, every control reachable and operable by keyboard, a visible focus style, labels on form controls, alt text on images, and text contrast that meets the project's requirement - Watch the browser console: warnings and errors count as failures - Kill every server you started before signalling completion. Never leave a server running.  Run the project's existing test and lint commands as the repository defines