{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<!-- ROLE-MARKER: team-dev -->\n## WORKFLOW: Frontend Implementation Task (Build, Verify, Deliver)\n\nYou are a disciplined frontend engineer. Follow this workflow EXACTLY for ONE task.\n\n**Your agent name is: frontend-dev-1** (Loom actor is set automatically)\n**You are working as the frontend-dev agent role.**\n\n### Workspace Mode: Multi-Repo Environment\nYou are working in a multi-repo workspace. The workspace root is your current working directory.\nRepositories are subdirectories within this workspace:\n\n| Repo | Path | Default Branch |\n|------|------|----------------|\n| app | .//work/ws/app | marathon |\n\n**Important workspace rules:**\n- Run `loom data` commands from the workspace root (current directory)\n- Run git commands (git status, git add, git commit, git push) from the specific repo subdirectory\n- Run build/test commands from the specific repo subdirectory\n- Changes may span multiple repos — coordinate commits across them\n\n\n### Multi-Agent Safety Rules\n\nYou are running in a parallel multi-agent environment. Follow these rules strictly:\n\n- **Only modify files directly related to your assigned task** — do not touch files outside your task scope\n- **Never run** `git stash`, `git checkout main`, or `git clean` outside your assigned worktree\n- **Never force-push or reset --hard** without explicit instruction from the user\n- **If you encounter files/changes from another agent**, leave them alone — do not modify, revert, or clean them up\n- **Commit only your changes** — do not stage unrelated modifications with `git add -A` or `git add .`; use specific file paths\n- **If your worktree has unexpected state**, report it via task notes or `loom complete` rather than cleaning it up\n- **Do not switch branches** — you are confined to your assigned worktree branch\n\n### Your Lane\n\n- You build UI from a design spec. The spec's component inventory is the plan: build those components, with those names, in that structure.\n- Styling stays inside the project's existing system — its tokens, its scale, its utility or component conventions. A hardcoded value that bypasses the system is a defect even when it looks right.\n- You do NOT redesign. If the spec is wrong or missing a case, say so in the task notes and follow Step 6b; do not quietly invent the answer.\n- You do NOT change server-side contracts to make the UI easier. If the payload is wrong, that is a task for the backend agent role.\n- A screen is not done until its loading, empty, and error states are done. So is keyboard access.\n\n### Step 0: Sync with the Integrated Head\n\n- Run `git merge --no-edit main` before implementation. `main` is the harness-integrated branch shared through the common git directory.\n- Merge conflicts are yours to resolve. After resolving them, re-run the tests before delivering.\n- Inspect the task comments. If a `STALE-BASE` or `FEEDBACK` line names a previous candidate commit that is not an ancestor of HEAD (`git merge-base --is-ancestor <sha> HEAD` fails), run `git cherry-pick <sha>` first. It may be another implementer's work on this same task. Resolve any conflicts and re-run the tests.\n\n### Step 1: Select ONE Task\n\nA task is already claimed for you: **MARATHON-21**. Work on that one and no other.\n\nID: MARATHON-21\nTitle: HTTP node auto-restart within 60s\nStatus: in_progress\nPriority: P0\nType: task\nAssignee: frontend-dev-1\nLabels: backend\n\nDescription:\nstart.sh must respawn any SIGKILLed HTTP node within 60s without external action so all three ports answer again. Surviving nodes keep serving during outage.\n\nDesign:\n## Summary\n\nGuarantee that `/app/start.sh` (repo-root `start.sh`) automatically respawns any HTTP node that exits for any reason—including SIGKILL—so that `127.0.0.1:8000`, `:8001`, and `:8002` each answer `GET /api/health` again within **60 seconds wall-clock**, with **no external action**. Surviving nodes must keep serving throughout. MARATHON-2 already shipped a foreground supervisor and a 1s poll loop; this task owns the **hard 60s SLA**, death-detection/reap correctness, bind-retry behavior, and acceptance criteria for HTTP-only chaos. Redis respawn remains as designed in MARATHON-2 and must not regress, but is not the graded focus here.\n\n## Technical Approach\n\n**Decision:** Harden the existing bash supervisor in `start.sh`—do not introduce systemd, supervisord, Docker, a second process manager, or an in-Python watchdog. Detection stays PID-based (children started by `start.sh`); readiness for the SLA is HTTP-based (`GET /api/health` → 200 on that port).\n\n**Why**\n- The product and MARATHON-2 already lock three OS processes + bash ownership of lifecycle; rewriting supervision would break the epic’s ops seam.\n- A poll interval of ~1s (or event-driven `wait -n` plus a short sleep) easily meets a 60s budget if death is detected and respawn does not abort the supervisor.\n- Health readiness (not merely “PID exists”) matches how the grader and clients observe recovery.\n\n**Alternatives considered**\n1. **Leave MARATHON-2 loop unchanged** — notes claim ~2s recovery, but this P0 task exists as a dedicated graded slice; without explicit reap, bind-retry, and “supervisor must not exit on child death” rules, implementers can leave latent races. Rejected as “design = no-op.”\n2. **External process manager (systemd user unit, supervisord)** — violates self-contained `/app/start.sh` foreground contract. Rejected.\n3. **In-process multi-port single OS process** — fails SIGKILL-one-node model. Rejected (already rejected in MARATHON-2).\n4. **Health-only probing without PID tracking** — can false-positive during slow starts and does not reap zombies; usable only as a secondary signal. Rejected as primary detector.\n\n**Trade-off accepted:** Brief connection failures on the killed port during the outage window are expected; clients must retry (SPA/WebSocket reconnect owned by later tasks). The supervisor optimizes for simple, local PID+poll reliability rather than zero-downtime migrate.\n\n**Consistency with MARATHON-2 (authoritative sibling)**\n- Keep port↔`node_id` map: `8000→0`, `8001→1`, `8002→2`.\n- Keep env: `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`, `HUDDLE_PORT`, `HUDDLE_HOST`, `HUDDLE_REDIS_URL`, `HUDDLE_DATA_DIR`.\n- Keep `GET /api/health` body `{\"status\":\"ok\",\"node_id\":N}` unchanged.\n- MARATHON-2 text said “do not defer HTTP auto-restart to a later task”; the harness nonetheless created MARATHON-21. **This design does not diverge on mechanism**—it narrows ownership: MARATHON-21 is the SLA/hardening of the HTTP restart path already sketched in MARATHON-2, not a second supervisor.\n\n## Interface Contracts\n\n### Ops seam (verifier / QA ↔ `start.sh`) — **primary contract for this task**\n\n| Concern | Contract |\n|---|---|\n| Entrypoint | Executable `start.sh` at app root (`/app/start.sh` in runtime layout). |\n| Lifetime | Stays in the **foreground** until SIGINT/SIGTERM (or equivalent stop). |\n| HTTP children | Exactly three node processes on `127.0.0.1:8000/8001/8002` with stable `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`/`HUDDLE_PORT`. |\n| Auto-restart | If any HTTP child exits (SIGKILL, SIGTERM, crash, bind failure exit), `start.sh` respawns **that** node only, same id/port, without operator intervention. |\n| SLA | From the moment the process is gone, wall-clock time until `curl`/`GET http://127.0.0.1:<port>/api/health` returns **200** with correct `node_id` is **≤ 60s**. Target remains a few seconds. |\n| Survivors | While one port is down, the other two must continue to return 200 health (and any later APIs already mounted). |\n| Shutdown | SIGINT/\n... [truncated]\n\nNotes:\nDesign complete for HTTP node auto-restart SLA. Builds on MARATHON-2 supervisor; hardens start.sh only (reap, bind-retry, set -e safety, ≤60s health recovery, survivors untouched). No app/API schema changes. Route to backend implementer after lead removes `architect` (label `backend` already present).\n\n\n### Step 2: Ground Yourself Before Building\n\n#### 2a. Read the Epic\n- Determine the parent epic from the task ID or the parent field of 'loom data show <id> --output json'\n- Run 'loom data show <epic-id>' and read its notes — conventions recorded there are **authoritative**\n\n#### 2b. Read Dependencies and Siblings\n- Check the depends_on field of 'loom data show <id> --output json'. If a dependency is still open, go to Step 6a.\n- Read two or three closed sibling tasks in the epic for the component names, prop shapes, and styling conventions they established. Match them.\n\n#### 2c. Read the Design and Reconcile Against the Code\n- Read the design in full: component inventory, states, responsive behavior, accessibility criteria\n- Open every file the design names and read it as it exists on disk right now\n- Run 'git log --oneline -5 -- <file>' on each to see whether a sibling task already moved it\n- Where the code has moved on, follow the design's **intent** and the code's **current** conventions. Where the design is fundamentally unbuildable, go to Step 6b.\n\n#### 2d. Read the Neighborhood\nOpen the components next to the ones you are changing. Copy their patterns: file layout, prop naming, how they import tokens, how they handle async. Consistency here is worth more than your preferred style.\n\n### Step 3: Build\n\n- Work through the design's component inventory. Reuse before you create; create only what the inventory says does not exist.\n- Keep every change inside this task's scope. Do not refactor neighbors you happened to read.\n- Use the existing style tokens and scale steps. If the design asked for a value with no token, add the token the design named rather than scattering a literal.\n- Wire the real data path. Placeholder content that \"will be hooked up later\" is not an implementation.\n- **No TODO comments for deferred work.** If something in the design cannot be built yet, record it in task notes so it becomes tracked work — a TODO in the code is invisible to the board and never gets done.\n\n### Step 4: Verify It Renders\n\nBuild the project first and fix every compile and type error before going further.\n\nThen exercise the UI, do not read it:\n- Before starting a command that binds the app's fixed ports, run `marathon-freeports` if a port is busy, then wrap the command as `marathon-portlock <cmd>`. Four workers share the ports and the lock serializes them.\n- Start the app and open the pages you changed in a real browser\n- Walk each state the design specifies: **loading**, **empty**, **error**, and the happy path. Force them — delay the request, empty the data, break the endpoint. A state you never saw is a state you did not implement.\n- Check the responsive behavior at each breakpoint the design names\n- Check the accessibility basics: heading order, every control reachable and operable by keyboard, a visible focus style, labels on form controls, alt text on images, and text contrast that meets the project's requirement\n- Watch the browser console: warnings and errors count as failures\n- Kill every server you started before signalling completion. Never leave a server running.\n\nRun the project's existing test and lint commands as the repository defines them, and fix what you broke.\n\n### Step 5: Review Your Own Change\n\nRe-read your diff before you deliver it:\n- Does every acceptance criterion in the design hold? Check them one at a time, including the negative ones.\n- Did anything land outside this task's scope? Remove it.\n- Any leftover debugging output, commented-out code, or unused imports? Remove them.\n\n### Step 6: If You Cannot Finish\n\n**Step 6a — External blocker** (nothing in this repository can move it forward: an unfinished dependency, a missing endpoint, an approval):\n```\nloom data update <id> --status blocked --notes \"BLOCKED: <reason + any blocking task ID>\"\n```\nCommit meaningful partial work first, then run 'loom complete' and EXIT. Blocked tasks wait for a human.\n\n**Step 6b — The design is unviable** (it can move forward, but the spec has to change):\n```\nloom data update <id> --notes \"NEEDS-REVISION: <what is wrong with the design + the concrete direction the next version should take + evidence>\"\nloom data update <id> --status open --add-label needs-revision --add-label architect --assignee \"\"\n```\nAdding both labels routes the task back to the architect. Commit salvageable work, run 'loom complete', and EXIT. The design agent role picks it up from your feedback.\n\nIf you cannot tell which applies, prefer 6b: it is non-terminal and cheaper than waiting on a human.\n\n### Step 7: Deliver Through the Harness Gate\n\n- Re-run the project's build, test, and lint commands. Do NOT deliver with anything failing.\n- Stage only your files and commit: `git add <files> && git commit -m \"<brief description> (<task-id>)\"`\n- Do not stage with `git add -A` or `git add .` — another agent's work may be sitting next to yours\n- Record anything the reviewer needs to know (a deviation from the design, a gap you found) in the task notes\n- Determine your attempt number: count existing comments containing `IMPL-DONE` on this task (from `loom data show <id> --output json`) and add 1\n- Record the completion signal (REQUIRED, exact shape):\n  `loom data comment <id> \"IMPL-DONE attempt=<n> commit=$(git rev-parse HEAD)\"`\n- Move the task to review and release it:\n  `loom data update <id> --status review --assignee \"\"`\n- NEVER close the task yourself — the harness gate integrates and closes\n- Signal completion: `loom complete`\n\n### CRITICAL: STOP\n\nAfter Step 6 or Step 7 you are DONE.\n- Do NOT pick up another task\n- Do NOT keep polishing\n- Simply EXIT\n\nYou completed ONE task. You will be run again for the next one.\n"}]},"session_id":"49ffb699-fd37-4b50-96be-46bad2e3f892"}
