gn\": \"## Summary\\n\\nGuarantee that `/app/start.sh` (repo-root `start.sh`) automatically respawns any HTTP node that exits for any reason—including SIGKILL—so that `127.0.0.1:8000`, `:8001`, and `:8002` each answer `GET /api/health` again within **60 seconds wall-clock**, with **no external action**. Surviving nodes must keep serving throughout. MARATHON-2 already shipped a foreground supervisor and a 1s poll loop; this task owns the **hard 60s SLA**, death-detection/reap correctness, bind-retry behavior, and acceptance criteria for HTTP-only chaos. Redis respawn remains as designed in MARATHON-2 and must not regress, but is not the graded focus here.\\n\\n## Technical Approach\\n\\n**Decision:** Harden the existing bash supervisor in `start.sh`—do not introduce systemd, supervisord, Docker, a second process manager, or an in-Python watchdog. Detection stays PID-based (children started by `start.sh`); readiness for the SLA is HTTP-based (`GET /api/health` → 200 on that port).\\n\\n**Why**\\n- The product and MARATHON-2 already lock three OS processes + bash ownership of lifecycle; rewriting supervision would break the epic’s ops seam.\\n- A poll interval of ~1s (or event-driven `wait -n` plus a short sleep) easily meets a 60s budget if death is detected and respawn does not abort the supervisor.\\n- Health readiness (not merely “PID exists”) matches how the grader and clients observe recovery.\\n\\n**Alternatives considered**\\n1. **Leave MARATHON-2 loop unchanged** — notes claim ~2s recovery, but this P0 task exists as a dedicated graded slice; without explicit reap, bind-retry, and “supervisor must not exit on child death” rules, implementers can leave latent races. Rejected as “design = no-op.”\\n2. **External process manager (systemd user unit, supervisord)** — violates self-contained `/app/start.sh` foreground contract. Rejected.\\n3. **In-process multi-port single OS process** — fails SIGKILL-one-node model. Rejected (already rejected in MARATHON-2).\\n4. **Health-only probing without PID tracking** — can false-positive during slow starts and does not reap zombies; usable only as a secondary signal. Rejected as primary detector.\\n\\n**Trade-off accepted:** Brief connection failures on the killed port during the outage window are expected; clients must retry (SPA/WebSocket reconnect owned by later tasks). The supervisor optimizes for simple, local PID+poll reliability rather than zero-downtime migrate.\\n\\n**Consistency with MARATHON-2 (authoritative sibling)**\\n- Keep port↔`node_id` map: `8000→0`, `8001→1`, `8002→2`.\\n- Keep env: `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`, `HUDDLE_PORT`, `HUDDLE_HOST`, `HUDDLE_REDIS_URL`, `HUDDLE_DATA_DIR`.\\n- Keep `GET /api/health` body `{\\\"status\\\":\\\"ok\\\",\\\"node_id\\\":N}` unchanged.\\n- MARATHON-2 text said “do not defer HTTP auto-restart to a later task”; the harness nonetheless created MARATHON-21. **This design does not diverge on mechanism**—it narrows ownership: MARATHON-21 is the SLA/hardening of the HTTP restart path already sketched in MARATHON-2, not a second supervisor.\\n\\n## Interface Contracts\\n\\n### Ops seam (verifier / QA ↔ `start.sh`) — **primary contract for this task**\\n\\n| Concern | Contract |\\n|---|---|\\n| Entrypoint | Executable `start.sh` at app root (`/app/start.sh` in runtime layout). |\\n| Lifetime | Stays in the **foreground** until SIGINT/SIGTERM (or equivalent stop). |\\n| HTTP children | Exactly three node processes on `127.0.0.1:8000/8001/8002` with stable `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`/`HUDDLE_PORT`. |\\n| Auto-restart | If any HTTP child exits (SIGKILL, SIGTERM, crash, bind failure exit), `start.sh` respawns **that** node only, same id/port, without operator intervention. |\\n| SLA | From the moment the process is gone, wall-clock time until `curl`/`GET http://127.0.0.1:\\u003cport\\u003e/api/health` returns **200** with correct `node_id` is **≤ 60s**. Target remains a few seconds. |\\n| Survivors | While one port is down, the other two must continue to return 200 health (and any later APIs already mounted). |\\n| Shutdown | SIGINT/SIGTERM to `start.sh` still tears down children (MARATHON-2); restart loop must not treat intentional shutdown as a respawn storm after trap runs. |\\n\\n**Ownership:** Backend / ops only. No frontend change in this task.\\n\\n### HTTP seam — `GET /api/health` (unchanged from MARATHON-2)\\n\\n| Side | Responsibility |\\n|---|---|\\n| Server (each node) | Return `200` `{\\\"status\\\":\\\"ok\\\",\\\"node_id\\\":\\u003c0\\\\|1\\\\|2\\u003e}` when the process is listening; do not probe Redis/SQLite. |\\n| Client (grader, future SPA) | Treat TCP refuse / timeout / non-200 as “this node down”; **retry**. Do not require all three nodes for reads that can hit a live peer. |\\n| While killed node is down | Clients using that port see connection errors; clients on surviving ports are unaffected. |\\n| After respawn | Same port, same `node_id`; no session stickiness invented here. |\\n\\n**Validation:** Server validates `HUDDLE_*` at process start (existing `huddle/config.py`). Client validates HTTP status + JSON keys for health checks.\\n\\n**Slow / fail behavior:** No new API errors. Outage is a transport failure, not an application error body.\\n\\n### Frontend / realtime seam (informational; not implemented here)\\n\\n| Consumer | Expected behavior during single-node kill |\\n|---|---|\\n| Future SPA (MARATHON-24+) | If its API base URL points at the dead port, show retry/reconnect; do not assume cluster-wide outage. |\\n| WebSocket (MARATHON-20) | Connection on dead node drops; resume/subscribe against a live node is that task’s problem. Restart only restores a peer process. |\\n| Cross-node fan-out (MARATHON-22) | May see a peer gap until respawn; must not require this task to change pub/sub code. |\\n\\nPayload shapes crossing the FE/BE JSON seam are **unchanged** by this task.\\n\\n## Data Model\\n\\nNo schema changes. No new entities, files under `data/`, or Redis key formats.\\n\\n| Store | Role for this task |\\n|---|---|\\n| In-supervisor state | Associative map `node_id → child PID` (existing `NODE_PIDS`); optional generation/restart counters for logs only. |\\n| SQLite / Redis data | Untouched; shared durable state must remain valid across respawn (already implied by shared `HUDDLE_DATA_DIR`). |\\n\\n**Backward compatible:** yes (behavior-only change to `start.sh`).\\n\\n## Files to Create / Files to Modify\\n\\n### Modify\\n\\n| File | Change |\\n|---|---|\\n| `start.sh` | Harden HTTP supervisor path: (1) **reap** exited node PIDs with non-blocking/`wait \\\"$pid\\\"` before clearing/`start_node`; (2) detect down via `kill -0` failure **after** reap attempt (and treat missing PID as down); (3) respawn only the dead `node_id`/`port` via existing `start_node`; (4) ensure child exit or failed bind **never** causes `set -e` to tear down the whole cluster—restart loop must continue until 60s SLA is met on retry; (5) keep poll cadence ≤ ~2s (current `sleep 1` is acceptable); (6) on post-kill port still busy, skip/fail that spawn attempt, log, retry next iteration—do not `die`; (7) preserve Redis supervision and INT/TERM trap from MARATHON-2; (8) log restart events with `node_id`, port, old/new PID. |\\n\\n### Do not create (unless implementer finds a tiny helper unavoidable)\\n\\n| File | Reason |\\n|---|---|\\n| New Python modules | No app-level restart API. |\\n| systemd / compose / Procfile | Forbidden pattern for this epic. |\\n| Changes to `huddle/api/health.py`, `huddle/config.py`, `huddle/app.py` | Contracts already correct; only touch if a proven bug blocks restart identity (unexpected). |\\n\\n### Create\\n\\nNone required.\\n\\n## Sequencing and Cross-Task Seams\\n\\n| Order | Dependency |\\n|---|---|\\n| Prerequisite | **MARATHON-2** (closed): cluster scaffold, health, basic supervisor — **done**. |\\n| This task | Can implement immediately; no dependency on auth/SPA/WS. |\\n| Parallel | Any API/SPA task may proceed; they must tolerate transient single-port refuse. |\\n| Downstream | MARATHON-20/22/23 assume peers can disappear and return; they consume the ops seam this task hardens but do not own it. |\\n\\n**Seams this task owns**\\n- Contract name: **HTTP node auto-restart SLA** (ops ↔ `start.sh`).\\n- Consumers: QA/grader, MARATHON-20 (WS), MARATHON-22 (fan-out), MARATHON-23 (IRC may be separate process—out of scope here).\\n\\n**Seams this task consumes**\\n- MARATHON-2: port map, `start_node` env contract, `GET /api/health`.\\n\\n**Split follow-ups?** No. Do not split Redis restart (already MARATHON-2) or hang detection (not required) into this ticket.\\n\\n## Out of Scope (Needs a Separate Task)\\n\\nNeighborhood scan (`start.sh`, `huddle/**`, `*.sh`, restart/supervisor greps):\\n\\n| Item | Why excluded |\\n|---|---|\\n| `timer.sh` | Harness countdown helper; unrelated to cluster supervision. |\\n| Redis auto-restart path in `start.sh` | Owned/graded under MARATHON-2; do not remove, but do not expand Redis semantics here. |\\n| `huddle/api/health.py` / config / app factory | Stable; no change required for restart. |\\n| IRC process on `:6667` (MARATHON-23) | Not an HTTP node; separate supervisor rules when that task lands. |\\n| WebSocket resume after peer death (MARATHON-20) | Client/protocol concern. |\\n| Redis-outage fan-out fallback (MARATHON-22) | Application messaging path, not process respawn. |\\n| SPA reconnect UX (MARATHON-24+) | Frontend. |\\n| Detecting hung-but-alive nodes (event loop deadlock) | Task specifies SIGKILL/exit respawn, not liveness beyond process death. |\\n| Load balancer / multi-host failover | Single-host three-port model only. |\\n| `loom-prompts/` | Agent prompts; not product code. |\\n\\n## Edge Cases \\u0026 Error Handling\\n\\n| Situation | Expected behavior |\\n|---|---|\\n| One HTTP child SIGKILL’d | Reap PID; restart that `node_id`/`port` only; other nodes uninterrupted; health on killed port back within ≤60s with same `node_id`. |\\n| All three SIGKILL’d in sequence | Restart each as detected; all three healthy again within 60s of each respective kill (not “60s for the batch only” as an excuse to delay the first). |\\n| Child exits immediately (bad env, import error) | Log error; retry on poll interval; **do not** exit `start.sh` after initial successful cluster start. (Fatal `die` remains allowed only during initial `ensure_deps` / first bind checks before the supervisor loop.) |\\n| Port still in `TIME_WAIT` / briefly busy after kill | Log; do not `die`; retry next loop iteration until bind succeeds; still meet 60s if OS releases promptly (typical). |\\n| Respawn gets a new PID | Update `NODE_PIDS[node_id]`; never reuse a stale PID for liveness. |\\n| Zombie / unreaped child | Always `wait` the recorded PID when down or after detected exit so the process table cannot fool later checks. |\\n| `kill -0` race (PID reused by unrelated process) | Prefer wait/reap bookkeeping; only start one child per `node_id`; optional sanity: if PID alive but port not listening for multiple polls, kill and restart **that** child only (implementer may add if needed to meet SLA; not required if PID tracking is correct). |\\n| Supervisor receives SIGINT/SIGTERM | Trap runs `stop_children`; exit; **no** respawn after shutdown begins. |\\n| Supervisor receives SIGKILL | Orphans possible; acceptable (MARATHON-2). |\\n| Redis down during HTTP restart | HTTP restart still proceeds; health must not depend on Redis (MARATHON-2). |\\n| Duplicate `start.sh` | Still fail fast on ports in use at initial start; out of scope to soft-adopt alien node PIDs. |\\n| Logging | Restarts go to stderr via existing `[huddle]` logger; no new log file required. |\\n| `set -euo pipefail` | Conditionals around `kill`/`wait`/`port` checks must be written so a down child cannot abort the loop. |\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. With cluster up via `./start.sh`, SIGKILL the PID of **one** HTTP node (the process serving one of 8000/8001/8002).\\n2. Continuously, the **other two** ports return `GET /api/health` → 200 with their correct `node_id` throughout the outage (sample at least once during the down window).\\n3. Within **60 seconds** of the kill, the killed port returns `GET /api/health` → 200 with the **same** `node_id` as before (8000→0, 8001→1, 8002→2).\\n4. No manual restart, no second terminal intervention, no re-running `start.sh`—the original foreground supervisor performs the respawn.\\n5. Repeat criteria 1–4 for each of the three ports (can be sequential).\\n6. Negative: killing one node must **not** cause `start.sh` to exit.\\n7. Negative: killing one node must **not** restart or kill the other two node PIDs as a side effect.\\n8. Negative: after respawn, `node_id` on port 8000 must **not** become 1 or 2 (identity stays port-derived).\\n9. Negative: recovery must **not** require Docker, remote services, or editing config mid-test.\\n10. `start.sh` remains foreground for the whole test; Redis PING still works unless Redis was also killed (Redis kill+respawn may be smoke-checked but is not the primary AC of this task).\\n\\n## Testing Strategy\\n\\n**Implementer**\\n- Chaos script: boot under `marathon-portlock` / free ports; record PIDs (e.g. from `ss`/`lsof` on each port or supervisor logs); `kill -9` one PID; poll health on all three ports every ~1s; assert survivors always 200; assert victim 200 by t≤60 with correct `node_id`.\\n- Repeat for each port; optionally double-kill two nodes and confirm both recover without killing the third intentionally.\\n- Regression: SIGINT to `start.sh` still shuts down children (no infinite respawn after trap).\\n- Confirm supervisor loop does not exit when a child returns non-zero.\\n\\n**Human / QA**\\n```bash\\nmarathon-freeports\\nmarathon-portlock ./start.sh\\n# other shell:\\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health\\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/health\\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8002/api/health\\n# identify PID listening on 8001; kill -9 $PID\\n# while true; do date; curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\\\n' http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health; \\\\\\n#   curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/health; sleep 1; done\\n# expect 8000/8002 healthy throughout; 8001 healthy again within 60s with node_id=1\\n```\\n\\n**Out of test scope:** SPA behavior, WS resume, IRC, auth, Redis fan-out fallback logic beyond “HTTP nodes stay up / return.”\",\n  \"design_format\": \"markdown\",\n  \"has_design\": true,\n  \"notes\": \"Design complete for HTTP node auto-restart SLA. Builds on MARATHON-2 supervisor; hardens start.sh only (reap, bind-retry, set -e safety, ≤60s health recovery, survivors untouched). No app/API schema changes. Route to backend implementer after lead removes `architect` (label `backend` already present).\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-08-22T18:37:57.516496742Z\",\n  \"updated_at\": \"2026-08-22T18:55:50.005182784Z\",\n  \"created_by\": \"marathon-harness\",\n  \"description\": \"start.sh must respawn any SIGKILLed HTTP node within 60s without external action so all three ports answer again. Surviving nodes keep serving during outage.\"\n}\n/work/ws/worktrees/app/frontend-dev-1\ntotal 36\ndrwxr-xr-x. 7 root root 4096 Aug 22 18:56 .\ndrwxr-xr-x. 6 root root   93 Aug 22 18:35 ..\n-rw-------. 1 root root  265 Aug 22 18:56 .agent.lock\n-rw-------. 1 root root    0 Aug 22 18:45 .agent.lock.flock\n-rw-r--r--. 1 root root   43 Aug 22 18:35 .git\n-rw-r--r--. 1 root root  135 Aug 22 18:47 .gitignore\ndrwxr-xr-x. 5 root root   74 Aug 22 18:46 .venv\ndrwxr-xr-x. 3 root root   35 Aug 22 18:46 data\ndrwxr-xr-x. 4 root root  105 Aug 22 18:46 huddle\ndrwxr-xr-x. 2 root root   34 Aug 22 18:35 loom-prompts\n-rw-r--r--. 1 root root   56 Aug 22 18:45 requirements.txt\n-rwxr-xr-x. 1 root root 4746 Aug 22 18:46 start.sh\n-rwxr-xr-x. 1 root root  558 Aug 22 18:35 timer.sh\ndrwxr-xr-x. 2 root root 4096 Aug 22 18:45 vendor\n","stderr":"time=2026-08-22T18:56:13.752Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:35473\n","executionTime":585,"interleavedOutput":"Already up to date.\ntime=2026-08-22T18:56:13.752Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:35473\n{\n  \"id\": \"MARATHON-21\",\n  \"title\": \"HTTP node auto-restart within 60s\",\n  \"status\": \"in_progress\",\n  \"priority\": 0,\n  \"issue_type\": \"task\",\n  \"assignee\": \"frontend-dev-1\",\n  \"owner\": \"marathon-harness\",\n  \"labels\": [\n    \"backend\"\n  ],\n  \"source_repo\": \"app\",\n  \"parent\": \"MARATHON-1\",\n  \"design\": \"## Summary\\n\\nGuarantee that `/app/start.sh` (repo-root `start.sh`) automatically respawns any HTTP node that exits for any reason—including SIGKILL—so that `127.0.0.1:8000`, `:8001`, and `:8002` each answer `GET /api/health` again within **60 seconds wall-clock**, with **no external action**. Surviving nodes must keep serving throughout. MARATHON-2 already shipped a foreground supervisor and a 1s poll loop; this task owns the **hard 60s SLA**, death-detection/reap correctness, bind-retry behavior, and acceptance criteria for HTTP-only chaos. Redis respawn remains as designed in MARATHON-2 and must not regress, but is not the graded focus here.\\n\\n## Technical Approach\\n\\n**Decision:** Harden the existing bash supervisor in `start.sh`—do not introduce systemd, supervisord, Docker, a second process manager, or an in-Python watchdog. Detection stays PID-based (children started by `start.sh`); readiness for the SLA is HTTP-based (`GET /api/health` → 200 on that port).\\n\\n**Why**\\n- The product and MARATHON-2 already lock three OS processes + bash ownership of lifecycle; rewriting supervision would break the epic’s ops seam.\\n- A poll interval of ~1s (or event-driven `wait -n` plus a short sleep) easily meets a 60s budget if death is detected and respawn does not abort the supervisor.\\n- Health readiness (not merely “PID exists”) matches how the grader and clients observe recovery.\\n\\n**Alternatives considered**\\n1. **Leave MARATHON-2 loop unchanged** — notes claim ~2s recovery, but this P0 task exists as a dedicated graded slice; without explicit reap, bind-retry, and “supervisor must not exit on child death” rules, implementers can leave latent races. Rejected as “design = no-op.”\\n2. **External process manager (systemd user unit, supervisord)** — violates self-contained `/app/start.sh` foreground contract. Rejected.\\n3. **In-process multi-port single OS process** — fails SIGKILL-one-node model. Rejected (already rejected in MARATHON-2).\\n4. **Health-only probing without PID tracking** — can false-positive during slow starts and does not reap zombies; usable only as a secondary signal. Rejected as primary detector.\\n\\n**Trade-off accepted:** Brief connection failures on the killed port during the outage window are expected; clients must retry (SPA/WebSocket reconnect owned by later tasks). The supervisor optimizes for simple, local PID+poll reliability rather than zero-downtime migrate.\\n\\n**Consistency with MARATHON-2 (authoritative sibling)**\\n- Keep port↔`node_id` map: `8000→0`, `8001→1`, `8002→2`.\\n- Keep env: `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`, `HUDDLE_PORT`, `HUDDLE_HOST`, `HUDDLE_REDIS_URL`, `HUDDLE_DATA_DIR`.\\n- Keep `GET /api/health` body `{\\\"status\\\":\\\"ok\\\",\\\"node_id\\\":N}` unchanged.\\n- MARATHON-2 text said “do not defer HTTP auto-restart to a later task”; the harness nonetheless created MARATHON-21. **This design does not diverge on mechanism**—it narrows ownership: MARATHON-21 is the SLA/hardening of the HTTP restart path already sketched in MARATHON-2, not a second supervisor.\\n\\n## Interface Contracts\\n\\n### Ops seam (verifier / QA ↔ `start.sh`) — **primary contract for this task**\\n\\n| Concern | Contract |\\n|---|---|\\n| Entrypoint | Executable `start.sh` at app root (`/app/start.sh` in runtime layout). |\\n| Lifetime | Stays in the **foreground** until SIGINT/SIGTERM (or equivalent stop). |\\n| HTTP children | Exactly three node processes on `127.0.0.1:8000/8001/8002` with stable `HUDDLE_NODE_ID`/`HUDDLE_PORT`. |\\n| Auto-restart | If any HTTP child exits (SIGKILL, SIGTERM, crash, bind failure exit), `start.sh` respawns **that** node only, same id/port, without operator intervention. |\\n| SLA | From the moment the process is gone, wall-clock time until `curl`/`GET http://127.0.0.1:\\u003cport\\u003e/api/health` returns **200** with correct `node_id` is **≤ 60s**. Target remains a few seconds. |\\n| Survivors | While one port is down, the other two must continue to return 200 health (and any later APIs already mounted). |\\n| Shutdown | SIGINT/SIGTERM to `start.sh` still tears down children (MARATHON-2); restart loop must not treat intentional shutdown as a respawn storm after trap runs. |\\n\\n**Ownership:** Backend / ops only. No frontend change in this task.\\n\\n### HTTP seam — `GET /api/health` (unchanged from MARATHON-2)\\n\\n| Side | Responsibility |\\n|---|---|\\n| Server (each node) | Return `200` `{\\\"status\\\":\\\"ok\\\",\\\"node_id\\\":\\u003c0\\\\|1\\\\|2\\u003e}` when the process is listening; do not probe Redis/SQLite. |\\n| Client (grader, future SPA) | Treat TCP refuse / timeout / non-200 as “this node down”; **retry**. Do not require all three nodes for reads that can hit a live peer. |\\n| While killed node is down | Clients using that port see connection errors; clients on surviving ports are unaffected. |\\n| After respawn | Same port, same `node_id`; no session stickiness invented here. |\\n\\n**Validation:** Server validates `HUDDLE_*` at process start (existing `huddle/config.py`). Client validates HTTP status + JSON keys for health checks.\\n\\n**Slow / fail behavior:** No new API errors. Outage is a transport failure, not an application error body.\\n\\n### Frontend / realtime seam (informational; not implemented here)\\n\\n| Consumer | Expected behavior during single-node kill |\\n|---|---|\\n| Future SPA (MARATHON-24+) | If its API base URL points at the dead port, show retry/reconnect; do not assume cluster-wide outage. |\\n| WebSocket (MARATHON-20) | Connection on dead node drops; resume/subscribe against a live node is that task’s problem. Restart only restores a peer process. |\\n| Cross-node fan-out (MARATHON-22) | May see a peer gap until respawn; must not require this task to change pub/sub code. |\\n\\nPayload shapes crossing the FE/BE JSON seam are **unchanged** by this task.\\n\\n## Data Model\\n\\nNo schema changes. No new entities, files under `data/`, or Redis key formats.\\n\\n| Store | Role for this task |\\n|---|---|\\n| In-supervisor state | Associative map `node_id → child PID` (existing `NODE_PIDS`); optional generation/restart counters for logs only. |\\n| SQLite / Redis data | Untouched; shared durable state must remain valid across respawn (already implied by shared `HUDDLE_DATA_DIR`). |\\n\\n**Backward compatible:** yes (behavior-only change to `start.sh`).\\n\\n## Files to Create / Files to Modify\\n\\n### Modify\\n\\n| File | Change |\\n|---|---|\\n| `start.sh` | Harden HTTP supervisor path: (1) **reap** exited node PIDs with non-blocking/`wait \\\"$pid\\\"` before clearing/`start_node`; (2) detect down via `kill -0` failure **after** reap attempt (and treat missing PID as down); (3) respawn only the dead `node_id`/`port` via existing `start_node`; (4) ensure child exit or failed bind **never** causes `set -e` to tear down the whole cluster—restart loop must continue until 60s SLA is met on retry; (5) keep poll cadence ≤ ~2s (current `sleep 1` is acceptable); (6) on post-kill port still busy, skip/fail that spawn attempt, log, retry next iteration—do not `die`; (7) preserve Redis supervision and INT/TERM trap from MARATHON-2; (8) log restart events with `node_id`, port, old/new PID. |\\n\\n### Do not create (unless implementer finds a tiny helper unavoidable)\\n\\n| File | Reason |\\n|---|---|\\n| New Python modules | No app-level restart API. |\\n| systemd / compose / Procfile | Forbidden pattern for this epic. |\\n| Changes to `huddle/api/health.py`, `huddle/config.py`, `huddle/app.py` | Contracts already correct; only touch if a proven bug blocks restart identity (unexpected). |\\n\\n### Create\\n\\nNone required.\\n\\n## Sequencing and Cross-Task Seams\\n\\n| Order | Dependency |\\n|---|---|\\n| Prerequisite | **MARATHON-2** (closed): cluster scaffold, health, basic supervisor — **done**. |\\n| This task | Can implement immediately; no dependency on auth/SPA/WS. |\\n| Parallel | Any API/SPA task may proceed; they must tolerate transient single-port refuse. |\\n| Downstream | MARATHON-20/22/23 assume peers can disappear and return; they consume the ops seam this task hardens but do not own it. |\\n\\n**Seams this task owns**\\n- Contract name: **HTTP node auto-restart SLA** (ops ↔ `start.sh`).\\n- Consumers: QA/grader, MARATHON-20 (WS), MARATHON-22 (fan-out), MARATHON-23 (IRC may be separate process—out of scope here).\\n\\n**Seams this task consumes**\\n- MARATHON-2: port map, `start_node` env contract, `GET /api/health`.\\n\\n**Split follow-ups?** No. Do not split Redis restart (already MARATHON-2) or hang detection (not required) into this ticket.\\n\\n## Out of Scope (Needs a Separate Task)\\n\\nNeighborhood scan (`start.sh`, `huddle/**`, `*.sh`, restart/supervisor greps):\\n\\n| Item | Why excluded |\\n|---|---|\\n| `timer.sh` | Harness countdown helper; unrelated to cluster supervision. |\\n| Redis auto-restart path in `start.sh` | Owned/graded under MARATHON-2; do not remove, but do not expand Redis semantics here. |\\n| `huddle/api/health.py` / config / app factory | Stable; no change required for restart. |\\n| IRC process on `:6667` (MARATHON-23) | Not an HTTP node; separate supervisor rules when that task lands. |\\n| WebSocket resume after peer death (MARATHON-20) | Client/protocol concern. |\\n| Redis-outage fan-out fallback (MARATHON-22) | Application messaging path, not process respawn. |\\n| SPA reconnect UX (MARATHON-24+) | Frontend. |\\n| Detecting hung-but-alive nodes (event loop deadlock) | Task specifies SIGKILL/exit respawn, not liveness beyond process death. |\\n| Load balancer / multi-host failover | Single-host three-port model only. |\\n| `loom-prompts/` | Agent prompts; not product code. |\\n\\n## Edge Cases \\u0026 Error Handling\\n\\n| Situation | Expected behavior |\\n|---|---|\\n| One HTTP child SIGKILL’d | Reap PID; restart that `node_id`/`port` only; other nodes uninterrupted; health on killed port back within ≤60s with same `node_id`. |\\n| All three SIGKILL’d in sequence | Restart each as detected; all three healthy again within 60s of each respective kill (not “60s for the batch only” as an excuse to delay the first). |\\n| Child exits immediately (bad env, import error) | Log error; retry on poll interval; **do not** exit `start.sh` after initial successful cluster start. (Fatal `die` remains allowed only during initial `ensure_deps` / first bind checks before the supervisor loop.) |\\n| Port still in `TIME_WAIT` / briefly busy after kill | Log; do not `die`; retry next loop iteration until bind succeeds; still meet 60s if OS releases promptly (typical). |\\n| Respawn gets a new PID | Update `NODE_PIDS[node_id]`; never reuse a stale PID for liveness. |\\n| Zombie / unreaped child | Always `wait` the recorded PID when down or after detected exit so the process table cannot fool later checks. |\\n| `kill -0` race (PID reused by unrelated process) | Prefer wait/reap bookkeeping; only start one child per `node_id`; optional sanity: if PID alive but port not listening for multiple polls, kill and restart **that** child only (implementer may add if needed to meet SLA; not required if PID tracking is correct). |\\n| Supervisor receives SIGINT/SIGTERM | Trap runs `stop_children`; exit; **no** respawn after shutdown begins. |\\n| Supervisor receives SIGKILL | Orphans possible; acceptable (MARATHON-2). |\\n| Redis down during HTTP restart | HTTP restart still proceeds; health must not depend on Redis (MARATHON-2). |\\n| Duplicate `start.sh` | Still fail fast on ports in use at initial start; out of scope to soft-adopt alien node PIDs. |\\n| Logging | Restarts go to stderr via existing `[huddle]` logger; no new log file required. |\\n| `set -euo pipefail` | Conditionals around `kill`/`wait`/`port` checks must be written so a down child cannot abort the loop. |\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. With cluster up via `./start.sh`, SIGKILL the PID of **one** HTTP node (the process serving one of 8000/8001/8002).\\n2. Continuously, the **other two** ports return `GET /api/health` → 200 with their correct `node_id` throughout the outage (sample at least once during the down window).\\n3. Within **60 seconds** of the kill, the killed port returns `GET /api/health` → 200 with the **same** `node_id` as before (8000→0, 8001→1, 8002→2).\\n4. No manual restart, no second terminal intervention, no re-running `start.sh`—the original foreground supervisor performs the respawn.\\n5. Repeat criteria 1–4 for each of the three ports (can be sequential).\\n6. Negative: killing one node must **not** cause `start.sh` to exit.\\n7. Negative: killing one node must **not** restart or kill the other two node PIDs as a side effect.\\n8. Negative: after respawn, `node_id` on port 8000 must **not** become 1 or 2 (identity stays port-derived).\\n9. Negative: recovery must **not** require Docker, remote services, or editing config mid-test.\\n10. `start.sh` remains foreground for the whole test; Redis PING still works unless Redis was also killed (Redis kill+respawn may be smoke-checked but is not the primary AC of this task).\\n\\n## Testing Strategy\\n\\n**Implementer**\\n- Chaos script: boot under `marathon-portlock` / free ports; record PIDs (e.g. from `ss`/`lsof` on each port or supervisor logs); `kill -9` one PID; poll health on all three ports every ~1s; assert survivors always 200; assert victim 200 by t≤60 with correct `node_id`.\\n- Repeat for each port; optionally double-kill two nodes and confirm both recover without killing the third intentionally.\\n- Regression: SIGINT to `start.sh` still shuts down children (no infinite respawn after trap).\\n- Confirm supervisor loop does not exit when a child returns non-zero.\\n\\n**Human / QA**\\n```bash\\nmarathon-freeports\\nmarathon-portlock ./start.sh\\n# other shell:\\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health\\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/health\\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8002/api/health\\n# identify PID listening on 8001; kill -9 $PID\\n# while true; do date; curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\\\n' http://127.0.0.1:8000/api/health; \\\\\\n#   curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8001/api/health; sleep 1; done\\n# expect 8000/8002 healthy throughout; 8001 healthy again within 60s with node_id=1\\n```\\n\\n**Out of test scope:** SPA behavior, WS resume, IRC, auth, Redis fan-out fallback logic beyond “HTTP nodes stay up / return.”\",\n  \"design_format\": \"markdown\",\n  \"has_design\": true,\n  \"notes\": \"Design complete for HTTP node auto-restart SLA. Builds on MARATHON-2 supervisor; hardens start.sh only (reap, bind-retry, set -e safety, ≤60s health recovery, survivors untouched). No app/API schema changes. Route to backend implementer after lead removes `architect` (label `backend` already present).\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-08-22T18:37:57.516496742Z\",\n  \"updated_at\": \"2026-08-22T18:55:50.005182784Z\",\n  \"created_by\": \"marathon-harness\",\n  \"description\": \"start.sh must respawn any SIGKILLed HTTP node within 60s without external action so all three ports answer again. Surviving nodes keep serving during outage.\"\n}\n/work/ws/worktrees/app/frontend-dev-1\ntotal 36\ndrwxr-xr-x. 7 root root 4096 Aug 22 18:56 .\ndrwxr-xr-x. 6 root root   93 Aug 22 18:35 ..\n-rw-------. 1 root root  265 Aug 22 18:56 .agent.lock\n-rw-------. 1 root root    0 Aug 22 18:45 .agent.lock.flock\n-rw-r--r--. 1 root root   43 Aug 22 18:35 .git\n-rw-r--r--. 1 root root  135 Aug 22 18:47 .gitignore\ndrwxr-xr-x. 5 root root   74 Aug 22 18:46 .venv\ndrwxr-xr-x. 3 root root   35 Aug 22 18:46 data\ndrwxr-xr-x. 4 root root  105 Aug 22 18:46 huddle\ndrwxr-xr-x. 2 root root   34 Aug 22 18:35 loom-prompts\n-rw-r--r--. 1 root root   56 Aug 22 18:45 requirements.txt\n-rwxr-xr-x. 1 root root 4746 Aug 22 18:46 start.sh\n-rwxr-xr-x. 1 root root  558 Aug 22 18:35 timer.sh\ndrwxr-xr-x. 2 root root 4096 Aug 22 18:45 vendor\n","localExecutionTimeMs":70},"isBackground":false},"description":"Merge main and show task JSON"},"hookAdditionalContexts":[],"toolCallId":"call-4e22f41a-c710-43d3-99fd-6e5fcdfcac66-0\nfc_024b68e5-f9a7-9938-8d33-816ad5c77f07_0","startedAtMs":"1787424973207","completedAtMs":"1787424973792"},"model_call_id":"e1004229-730f-4840-966d-b932d594bc6c-0-efy8","session_id":"49ffb699-fd37-4b50-96be-46bad2e3f892","timestamp_ms":1787424973820}
