stderr\")\n      --workspace string    Workspace ID. Env: LOOM_WORKSPACE\n\nUse \"loom data [command] --help\" for more information about a command.\ntime=2026-08-22T18:56:20.244Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:35473\nID:       MARATHON-21\nTitle:    HTTP node auto-restart within 60s\nStatus:   in_progress\nPriority: 0\nType:     task\nAssignee: frontend-dev-1\nOwner:    marathon-harness\nParent:   MARATHON-1\nLabels:   [backend]\n\nDescription:\nstart.sh must respawn any SIGKILLed HTTP node within 60s without external action so all three ports answer again. Surviving nodes keep serving during outage.\n\nDesign (--design):\n## Summary\n\nGuarantee that `/app/start.sh` (repo-root `start.sh`) automatically respawns any HTTP node that exits for any reason—including SIGKILL—so that `127.0.0.1:8000`, `:8001`, and `:8002` each answer `GET /api/health` again within **60 seconds wall-clock**, with **no external action**. Surviving nodes must keep serving throughout. MARATHON-2 already shipped a foreground supervisor and a 1s poll loop; this task owns the **hard 60s SLA**, death-detection/reap correctness, bind-retry behavior, and acceptance criteria for HTTP-only chaos. Redis respawn remains as designed in MARATHON-2 and must not regress, but is not the graded focus here.\n\n## Technical Approach\n\n**Decision:** Harden the existing bash supervisor in `start.sh`—do not introduce systemd, supervisord, Docker, a second process manager, or an in-Python watchdog. Detection stays PID-based (children started by `start.sh`); readiness for the SLA is HTTP-based (`GET /api/health` → 200 on that port).\n\n**Why**\n- The product and MARATHON-2 already lock three OS processes + bash ownership of lifecycle; rewriting supervision would break the epic’s ops seam.\n- A poll interval of ~1s (or event-driven `wait -n` plus a short sleep) easily meets a 60s budget if death is detected and respawn does not abort the supervisor.\n- Health readin